    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: (d)</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $a_1 + a_2\\cos(2x) + a_3\\sin^2(x) = 0$ for all $x$.<br/>Use $\\cos(2x) = 1 - 2\\sin^2(x)$:<br/>$a_1 + a_2(1 - 2\\sin^2(x)) + a_3\\sin^2(x) = 0$<br/>$(a_1 + a_2) + (a_3 - 2a_2)\\sin^2(x) = 0$<br/>For this to hold for all $x$, the coefficients must be zero:<br/>$a_1 + a_2 = 0 \\implies a_1 = -a_2$<br/>$a_3 - 2a_2 = 0 \\implies a_3 = 2a_2$<br/>The triplets are of the form $(-a_2, a_2, 2a_2)$. Since $a_2$ can be any real number, there are infinitely many such triplets.</p>",
