    "chapter": "3D Geometry",
    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: (a, b)</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>For points on $L$ to be at a constant distance from planes $P_1: x+2y-z+1=0$ and $P_2: 2x-y+z-1=0$, line $L$ must be parallel to both planes.<br/>Direction of $L = \\vec{n}_1 \\times \\vec{n}_2 = (1, 2, -1) \\times (2, -1, 1) = (1, -3, -5)$.<br/>$L$ passes through origin, so $L: \\vec{r} = t(1, -3, -5)$. Points on $L$ are $A(t, -3t, -5t)$.<br/>Let $F$ be the foot of perpendicular from $A$ to $P_1$. Line $AF$: $x = t+r, y = -3t+2r, z = -5t-r$.<br/>$F$ lies on $P_1$: $(t+r) + 2(-3t+2r) - (-5t-r) + 1 = 0 \\implies 6r + 1 = 0 \\implies r = -1/6$.<br/>So $F$ coordinates are $x = t - 1/6$, $y = -3t - 1/3$, $z = -5t + 1/6$. This is the locus $M$.<br/>Check option (a): $(0, -5/6, -2/3)$. $t - 1/6 = 0 \\implies t = 1/6$. Then $y = -3(1/6) - 1/3 = -5/6$, $z = -5(1/6) + 1/6 = -2/3$. Matches.<br/>Check option (b): $(-1/6, -1/3, 1/6)$. $t - 1/6 = -1/6 \\implies t = 0$. Then $y = -1/3, z = 1/6$. Matches.</p>",
