    "text": "${P}_{1}$ and ${P}_{2}$ are planes passing through origin. ${L}_{1}$ and ${L}_{2}$ are two lines on ${P}_{1}$ and ${P}_{2}$ respectively such that their intersection is origin. Show that there exists points $A,B$ , $C$ whose permutation ${A}^{'},{B}^{'},{C}^{'}$ can be chosen such that (i) $A$ is on ${L}_{1},B$ on ${P}_{1}$ but not on ${L}_{1}$ and $C$ not on ${P}_{1}$ (ii) ${A}^{'}$ is on ${L}_{2},{B}^{'}$ on ${P}_{2}$ but not on ${L}_{2}$ and ${C}^{'}$ not on ${P}_{2}$ .",
