    "solution": "<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Evaluating the items in Column I:<br/>(i) $\\tan^{-1}(\\frac{1}{2i^2}) = \\tan^{-1}(\\frac{2}{4i^2}) = \\tan^{-1}(\\frac{(2i+1) - (2i-1)}{1 + (2i-1)(2i+1)}) = \\tan^{-1}(2i+1) - \\tan^{-1}(2i-1)$.<br/>Summing from $i=1$ to $n$: $(\\tan^{-1}(3) - \\tan^{-1}(1)) + (\\tan^{-1}(5) - \\tan^{-1}(3)) + \\dots = \\tan^{-1}(2n+1) - \\tan^{-1}(1)$.<br/>As $n \\to \\infty$, this approaches $\\pi/2 - \\pi/4 = \\pi/4$. So $t = \\pi/4 \\implies \\tan t = 1$.<br/>(iii) Line is perpendicular to $x+2y+2z=0$ (so direction is $(1,2,2)$) and passes through $(0,1,0)$.<br/>A general point on the line is $P(\\lambda, 1+2\\lambda, 2\\lambda)$. Distance to origin $d = \\sqrt{\\lambda^2 + (1+2\\lambda)^2 + 4\\lambda^2} = \\sqrt{9\\lambda^2 + 4\\lambda + 1}$.<br/>Minimum distance occurs when $18\\lambda + 4 = 0 \\implies \\lambda = -2/9$.<br/>$d^2 = 9(-2/9)^2 + 4(-2/9) + 1 = 4/9 - 8/9 + 1 = 5/9$. So $d = \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$.<br/>(iv) Plane perpendicular to $2x-2y+z=0$ and $x-y+2z=4$. Normal $\\vec{n} = (2,-2,1) \\times (1,-1,2) = (-3, -3, 0)$.<br/>We can use $(1, 1, 0)$ as normal. Passing through $(1, -2, 1)$, plane is $1(x-1) + 1(y+2) = 0 \\implies x+y+1=0$.<br/>Distance from $(1,2,2)$ is $\\frac{|1+2+1|}{\\sqrt{1^2+1^2}} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}} = 2\\sqrt{2}$.</p>",
