    "solution": "<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let's find the intersection of $L_1$ and $L_2$.<br/>$L_1: (1+2\\lambda, -\\lambda, -3+\\lambda)$ and $L_2: (4+\\mu, -3+\\mu, -3+2\\mu)$.<br/>Equating coordinates: $1+2\\lambda = 4+\\mu \\implies 2\\lambda - \\mu = 3$<br/>$-\\lambda = -3+\\mu \\implies \\lambda + \\mu = 3$.<br/>Solving these gives $\\lambda = 2$ and $\\mu = 1$.<br/>Checking z-coordinate: $-3+2 = -1$ and $-3+2(1) = -1$. They intersect at $M(5, -2, -1)$.<br/>The required plane is perpendicular to $P_1$ and $P_2$. Its normal $\\vec{n} = \\vec{n}_1 \\times \\vec{n}_2$.<br/>$\\vec{n} = (7, 1, 2) \\times (3, 5, -6) = (-6 - 10, 6 - (-42), 35 - 3) = (-16, 48, 32) = 16(-1, 3, 2)$.<br/>Taking $\\vec{n} = (1, -3, -2)$, the plane through $(5, -2, -1)$ is:<br/>$1(x - 5) - 3(y + 2) - 2(z + 1) = 0 \\implies x - 3y - 2z = 5 + 6 + 2 = 13$.<br/>Comparing with $ax + by + cz = d$, we get $a=1, b=-3, c=-2, d=13$.<br/>Thus, (P) $\\to$ (3), (Q) $\\to$ (2), (R) $\\to$ (4), (S) $\\to$ (1).</p>",
