    "type": "single mcq",
    "text": "Let $\\gamma \\in R$ be such that the lines ${L}_{1}: \\frac{x+11}{1} = \\frac{y+21}{2} = \\frac{z+29}{3}$ and ${L}_{2}: \\frac{x+16}{3} = \\frac{y+11}{2} = \\frac{z+4}{\\gamma }$ intersect. Let ${R}_{1}$ be the point of intersection of ${L}_{1}$ and ${L}_{2}$ . Let $O=(0,0,0)$ , and $\\vec{n}$ denote a unit normal vector to the plane containing both the lines ${L}_{1}$ and ${L}_{2}$ . Match each entry in List-I to the correct entry in List-II. List-IList-IList-IIList-II(P)$\\gamma$ equals(1)$- \\vec{i} - \\vec{j} + \\vec{k}$(Q)A possible choice for $\\vec{n}$ is(2)$\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$(R)$\\vec{O{R}_{1}}$ equals(3)1(S)A possible value of $\\vec{O{R}_{1}} ⋅ \\vec{n}$ is(4)$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\vec{i} - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\vec{j} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\vec{k}$(5)$\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$ The correct option is",
