    "correct": "c",
    "chapter": "3D Geometry",
    "topic": "List-I   List-Ii",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$L_1: (-11+\\lambda, -21+2\\lambda, -29+3\\lambda)$ and $L_2: (-16+3\\mu, -11+2\\mu, -4+\\gamma\\mu)$.<br/>Intersecting coordinates: $-11+\\lambda = -16+3\\mu \\implies \\lambda - 3\\mu = -5$.<br/>$-21+2\\lambda = -11+2\\mu \\implies \\lambda - \\mu = 5$.<br/>Solving these gives $\\mu = 5$ and $\\lambda = 10$.<br/>For z-coordinate: $-29+3(10) = -4 + \\gamma(5) \\implies 1 = -4 + 5\\gamma \\implies 5\\gamma = 5 \\implies \\gamma = 1$.<br/>(P) $\\gamma = 1 \\implies$ (3).<br/>The intersection point $R_1$ is $(-11+10, -21+20, -29+30) = (-1, -1, 1)$.<br/>(R) $\\vec{OR_1} = -\\hat{i} - \\hat{j} + \\hat{k} \\implies$ (1).<br/>Normal $\\vec{n}_0$ to the plane containing $L_1, L_2$ is $(1,2,3) \\times (3,2,1) = (-4, 8, -4)$.<br/>Unit normal $\\vec{n} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(1, -2, 1)$ or its negative.<br/>(Q) A possible choice for $\\vec{n}$ is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\hat{i} - \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\hat{j} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\hat{k} \\implies$ (4).<br/>(S) $\\vec{OR_1} \\cdot \\vec{n} = (-1, -1, 1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(1, -2, 1) = \\frac{-1 + 2 + 1}{\\sqrt{6}} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\implies$ (5).</p>",
