    "chapter": "Application Of Calculus",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $f(x) = f(1-x)$.<br/>The area $R_2$ is defined as the integral of $f(x)$ from $x=-1$ to $x=2$:<br/>$R_2 = \\int_{-1}^2 f(x) dx$.<br/>The value $R_1$ is given by:<br/>$R_1 = \\int_{-1}^2 x f(x) dx$.<br/>We use the definite integral property $\\int_a^b f(x) dx = \\int_a^b f(a+b-x) dx$.<br/>Here, $a = -1, b = 2$, so $a+b-x = -1 + 2 - x = 1-x$.<br/>Applying this to $R_1$:<br/>$R_1 = \\int_{-1}^2 (1-x) f(1-x) dx$.<br/>Since $f(1-x) = f(x)$, we have:<br/>$R_1 = \\int_{-1}^2 (1-x) f(x) dx = \\int_{-1}^2 f(x) dx - \\int_{-1}^2 x f(x) dx$.<br/>Substituting $R_2$ and $R_1$ into the right side:<br/>$R_1 = R_2 - R_1$<br/>$2R_1 = R_2 \\implies R_1 = \\frac{R_2}{2}$.</p>",
