    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Consider the sum $S = \\sum_{i=0}^m \\binom{10}{i} \\binom{20}{m-i}$.<br/>We know from Vandermonde\\'s Identity that this sum represents the coefficient of $x^m$ in the expansion of $(1+x)^{10} (1+x)^{20}$.<br/>Since $(1+x)^{10} (1+x)^{20} = (1+x)^{30}$, the coefficient of $x^m$ is $\\binom{30}{m}$.<br/>Thus, $S = \\binom{30}{m}$.<br/>The binomial coefficient $\\binom{n}{r}$ is maximum when $r = n/2$ (if $n$ is even).<br/>Here, $n = 30$, which is even, so the maximum occurs at $m = 30/2 = 15$.<br/>Therefore, the maximum value is attained when $m = 15$.</p>",
