    "correct": "Check Answer Key",
    "chapter": "Binomial Theorem",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: Check Answer Key</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $S = \\sum_{r=1}^{2n} (-1)^{r-1} r \\binom{2n}{r}^2$.<br/>Using the property $r \\binom{n}{r} = n \\binom{n-1}{r-1}$, we rewrite $S$ as:<br/>$S = \\sum_{r=1}^{2n} (-1)^{r-1} 2n \\binom{2n-1}{r-1} \\binom{2n}{r} = 2n \\sum_{r=1}^{2n} (-1)^{r-1} \\binom{2n-1}{r-1} \\binom{2n}{2n-r}$.<br/>This sum represents the coefficient of $x^{2n-1}$ in the product of $(1 - x)^{2n-1}$ and $(1 + x)^{2n}$.<br/>Product: $(1 - x)^{2n-1} (1 + x)^{2n} = (1 - x)^{2n-1} (1 + x)^{2n-1} (1 + x) = (1 - x^2)^{2n-1} (1 + x) = (1 - x^2)^{2n-1} + x(1 - x^2)^{2n-1}$.<br/>We need the coefficient of $x^{2n-1}$.<br/>The term $(1 - x^2)^{2n-1}$ only contains even powers of $x$, so it contributes $0$ to the coefficient of $x^{2n-1}$.<br/>For $x(1 - x^2)^{2n-1}$, we need the coefficient of $x^{2n-2}$ in $(1 - x^2)^{2n-1}$.<br/>The general term of $(1 - x^2)^{2n-1}$ is $\\binom{2n-1}{k}(-x^2)^k$.<br/>Set $2k = 2n-2 \\implies k = n-1$.<br/>The coefficient is $(-1)^{n-1} \\binom{2n-1}{n-1}$.<br/>Therefore, $S = 2n (-1)^{n-1} \\binom{2n-1}{n-1}$.<br/>Notice that $2n \\binom{2n-1}{n-1} = 2n \\frac{(2n-1)!}{(n-1)! n!} = n \\frac{(2n)!}{n! n!} = n \\binom{2n}{n}$.<br/>Thus, $S = (-1)^{n-1} n \\binom{2n}{n}$.<br/>Hence proved.</p>",
