    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let the expression be $E = (1+i)^{n_1} + (1+i^3)^{n_1} + (1+i^5)^{n_2} + (1+i^7)^{n_2}$.<br/>We know that $i^3 = -i$, $i^5 = i$, and $i^7 = -i$.<br/>Substituting these into the expression:<br/>$E = (1+i)^{n_1} + (1-i)^{n_1} + (1+i)^{n_2} + (1-i)^{n_2}$.<br/>Notice that $(1-i)$ is the complex conjugate of $(1+i)$.<br/>So, $(1-i)^{n_1} = \\overline{(1+i)^{n_1}}$.<br/>Therefore, $(1+i)^{n_1} + (1-i)^{n_1} = 2 \\text{Re}((1+i)^{n_1})$, which is a real number.<br/>Similarly, $(1+i)^{n_2} + (1-i)^{n_2} = 2 \\text{Re}((1+i)^{n_2})$, which is also a real number.<br/>Thus, $E$ is the sum of two real numbers, so it is always real for any positive integers $n_1$ and $n_2$.<br/>This corresponds to option (d).</p>",
