    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $\\omega = -\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, which is a complex cube root of unity.<br/>The given expression becomes $4 + 5\\omega^{334} + 3\\omega^{365}$.<br/>Since $\\omega^3 = 1$, we can simplify the exponents by finding their remainder when divided by 3:<br/>$334 = 3 \\times 111 + 1 \\implies \\omega^{334} = \\omega^1 = \\omega$.<br/>$365 = 3 \\times 121 + 2 \\implies \\omega^{365} = \\omega^2$.<br/>The expression simplifies to $4 + 5\\omega + 3\\omega^2$.<br/>We know that $1 + \\omega + \\omega^2 = 0 \\implies \\omega^2 = -1 - \\omega$.<br/>Substitute this into the expression:<br/>$= 4 + 5\\omega + 3(-1 - \\omega) = 4 + 5\\omega - 3 - 3\\omega = 1 + 2\\omega$.<br/>Now substitute $\\omega = -\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{i\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ back:<br/>$= 1 + 2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = 1 - 1 + i\\sqrt{3} = i\\sqrt{3}$.</p>",
