    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $(1 + \\omega^2)^n = (1 + \\omega^4)^n$.<br/>Since $\\omega$ is a cube root of unity, $1 + \\omega + \\omega^2 = 0 \\implies 1 + \\omega^2 = -\\omega$.<br/>Also, $\\omega^4 = \\omega$, so $1 + \\omega^4 = 1 + \\omega = -\\omega^2$.<br/>Substitute these into the equation:<br/>$(-\\omega)^n = (-\\omega^2)^n$<br/>$(-1)^n \\omega^n = (-1)^n \\omega^{2n}$<br/>Assuming $n$ is a positive integer, we can divide both sides by $(-1)^n \\omega^n$ to get:<br/>$\\omega^n = 1$.<br/>Since $\\omega$ is a primitive cube root of unity, the least positive integer $n$ satisfying $\\omega^n = 1$ is $n = 3$.</p>",
