    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $z = e^{i\\theta}$ since $|z| = 1$. The condition $z \\neq \\pm 1$ means $\\theta \\neq n\\pi$.<br/>Consider the expression $w = \\frac{z}{1 - z^2}$.<br/>Substitute $z = e^{i\\theta}$:<br/>$w = \\frac{e^{i\\theta}}{1 - e^{2i\\theta}} = \\frac{e^{i\\theta}}{e^{i\\theta}(e^{-i\\theta} - e^{i\\theta})} = \\frac{1}{e^{-i\\theta} - e^{i\\theta}}$.<br/>We know that $e^{i\\theta} - e^{-i\\theta} = 2i \\sin\\theta$.<br/>Therefore, $e^{-i\\theta} - e^{i\\theta} = -2i \\sin\\theta$.<br/>Substitute this back into $w$:<br/>$w = \\frac{1}{-2i \\sin\\theta} = \\frac{i}{2\\sin\\theta}$.<br/>Since $\\theta \\neq n\\pi$, $\\sin\\theta \\neq 0$, and $w$ is a purely imaginary number.<br/>Thus, all values of $\\frac{z}{1-z^2}$ lie on the imaginary axis.</p>",
