    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The particle starts at $z_0 = 1 + 2i$.<br/>It moves horizontally by 5 units and vertically by 3 units (assuming positive directions as per standard convention for such problems):<br/>$z_1 = z_0 + 5 + 3i = 1 + 2i + 5 + 3i = 6 + 5i$.<br/>Next, it moves $\\sqrt{2}$ units in the direction of the vector $\\hat{i} + \\hat{j}$. The unit vector in this direction is $\\frac{1 + i}{\\sqrt{2}}$.<br/>The displacement is $\\sqrt{2} \\times \\frac{1 + i}{\\sqrt{2}} = 1 + i$.<br/>So, the new position is $z\\' = z_1 + 1 + i = 6 + 5i + 1 + i = 7 + 6i$.<br/>Finally, it moves through an angle $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ in the anticlockwise direction on a circle centered at the origin.<br/>This corresponds to multiplying the complex number by $e^{i\\pi/2} = i$.<br/>So, $z_2 = z\\' \\cdot i = (7 + 6i)i = 7i + 6i^2 = -6 + 7i$.<br/>This corresponds to option (d).</p>",
