    "correct": "c, d",
    "chapter": "Complex Number",
    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c, d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We have $w = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} + i}{2} = e^{i\\pi/6}$.<br/>The set $P = \\{w^n : n = 1, 2, \\dots\\}$ consists of points on the unit circle at angles $n\\pi/6$.<br/>$H_1$ is the region $\\text{Re}(z) > 1/2$. The points in $P \\cap H_1$ have $\\cos(n\\pi/6) > 1/2$.<br/>This happens for $n = 1$ ($\\pi/6$), $n = 11$ ($11\\pi/6$), and $n = 12$ ($0$).<br/>$H_2$ is the region $\\text{Re}(z) < -1/2$. The points in $P \\cap H_2$ have $\\cos(n\\pi/6) < -1/2$.<br/>This happens for $n = 5$ ($5\\pi/6$), $n = 7$ ($7\\pi/6$), and $n = 6$ ($\\pi$).<br/>The angle $\\angle z_1 O z_2$ is the absolute difference between their arguments, $\\min(|\\arg(z_1) - \\arg(z_2)|, 2\\pi - |\\arg(z_1) - \\arg(z_2)|)$.<br/>Choosing $z_1 = e^{i\\pi/6}$ and $z_2 = e^{i5\\pi/6}$, the angle is $5\\pi/6 - \\pi/6 = 4\\pi/6 = 2\\pi/3$. (Option c).<br/>Choosing $z_1 = e^{i\\pi/6}$ and $z_2 = e^{i\\pi}$, the angle is $\\pi - \\pi/6 = 5\\pi/6$. (Option d).<br/>Thus, possible angles are $2\\pi/3$ and $5\\pi/6$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Easy"
