    "chapter": "Definite Integrals",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We need to evaluate $I = \\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2} [2\\sin x] dx$.<br/>Let\\'s break the interval $[\\pi/2, 3\\pi/2]$ into sub-intervals where $[2\\sin x]$ is constant.<br/>1. $x \\in [\\pi/2, 5\\pi/6]$: $\\sin x$ goes from 1 to $1/2$, so $2\\sin x$ goes from 2 to 1. Thus, $[2\\sin x] = 1$ (except at $\\pi/2$ where it is 2).<br/>2. $x \\in (5\\pi/6, \\pi]$: $\\sin x$ goes from $1/2$ to 0, so $2\\sin x$ goes from 1 to 0. Thus, $[2\\sin x] = 0$.<br/>3. $x \\in (\\pi, 7\\pi/6]$: $\\sin x$ goes from 0 to $-1/2$, so $2\\sin x$ goes from 0 to -1. Thus, $[2\\sin x] = -1$.<br/>4. $x \\in (7\\pi/6, 3\\pi/2]$: $\\sin x$ goes from $-1/2$ to -1, so $2\\sin x$ goes from -1 to -2. Thus, $[2\\sin x] = -2$ (except at $3\\pi/2$ where it is -2).<br/>Now we integrate over these sub-intervals:<br/>$I = \\int_{\\pi/2}^{5\\pi/6} 1 dx + \\int_{5\\pi/6}^{\\pi} 0 dx + \\int_{\\pi}^{7\\pi/6} -1 dx + \\int_{7\\pi/6}^{3\\pi/2} -2 dx$<br/>$I = \\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6} - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + 0 - \\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6} - \\pi\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2} - \\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)$<br/>$I = \\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{6}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right) - 2\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{6}\\right)$<br/>$I = \\frac{\\pi}{3} - \\frac{\\pi}{6} - \\frac{2\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{\\pi}{3} - \\frac{\\pi}{6} = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
