    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The function is defined piecewise:<br/>$f(x) = e^{\\cos x}\\sin x$ for $x \\in [-2, 2]$<br/>$f(x) = 2$ for $x \\notin [-2, 2]$.<br/>We need to evaluate $I = \\int_{-2}^3 f(x) dx$.<br/>We can split the integral:<br/>$I = \\int_{-2}^2 f(x) dx + \\int_2^3 f(x) dx$<br/>$I = \\int_{-2}^2 e^{\\cos x}\\sin x dx + \\int_2^3 2 dx$<br/>Let $h(x) = e^{\\cos x}\\sin x$. Let\\'s check if it is odd or even.<br/>$h(-x) = e^{\\cos(-x)}\\sin(-x) = e^{\\cos x}(-\\sin x) = -e^{\\cos x}\\sin x = -h(x)$.<br/>Since $h(x)$ is an odd function, its integral over the symmetric interval $[-2, 2]$ is zero:<br/>$\\int_{-2}^2 e^{\\cos x}\\sin x dx = 0$.<br/>Now evaluate the second integral:<br/>$\\int_2^3 2 dx = [2x]_2^3 = 2(3 - 2) = 2(1) = 2$.<br/>Adding them together: $I = 0 + 2 = 2$.</p>",
