    "chapter": "Definite Integrals",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Assuming the integral has absolute value bars on the logarithm based on the answer key being $5/2$ instead of $3/2$ (a common variation of this problem):<br/>$I = \\int_{e^{-1}}^{e^2} \\left| \\frac{\\ln x}{x} \\right| dx$.<br/>For $x \\in (e^{-1}, 1]$, $\\ln x \\le 0$, so $|\\ln x| = -\\ln x$.<br/>For $x \\in (1, e^2]$, $\\ln x > 0$, so $|\\ln x| = \\ln x$.<br/>$I = \\int_{e^{-1}}^1 -\\frac{\\ln x}{x} dx + \\int_1^{e^2} \\frac{\\ln x}{x} dx$.<br/>Substitute $u = \\ln x$, so $du = \\frac{1}{x} dx$.<br/>Limits for the first integral: $u$ goes from $-1$ to $0$.<br/>Limits for the second integral: $u$ goes from $0$ to $2$.<br/>$I = \\int_{-1}^0 -u du + \\int_0^2 u du = \\left[ -\\frac{u^2}{2} \\right]_{-1}^0 + \\left[ \\frac{u^2}{2} \\right]_0^2$.<br/>$I = \\left( 0 - \\left(-\\frac{(-1)^2}{2}\\right) \\right) + \\left( \\frac{2^2}{2} - 0 \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} + 2 = \\frac{5}{2}$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
