    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $F(x) = \\int_0^x f(t) dt$ and $F(x^2) = x^2(1 + x) = x^2 + x^3$.<br/>We want to find $f(4)$.<br/>Let $u = x^2$. Since $x \\in (0, \\infty)$, $x = \\sqrt{u}$.<br/>Substitute $x = \\sqrt{u}$ into the given equation:<br/>$F(u) = (\\sqrt{u})^2 + (\\sqrt{u})^3 = u + u^{3/2}$.<br/>By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, $F\\'(u) = f(u)$.<br/>So, we differentiate $F(u)$ with respect to $u$:<br/>$f(u) = F\\'(u) = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}u^{1/2} = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{u}$.<br/>To find $f(4)$, we plug in $u = 4$:<br/>$f(4) = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{4} = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}(2) = 1 + 3 = 4$.</p>",
