    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $I = \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{1 + a^x} dx$.<br/>Use the property $\\int_{-a}^a f(x) dx = \\int_{-a}^a f(-x) dx$:<br/>$I = \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi \\frac{\\cos^2(-x)}{1 + a^{-x}} dx = \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{1 + 1/a^x} dx = \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi \\frac{a^x \\cos^2 x}{a^x + 1} dx$.<br/>Add the two expressions for $I$:<br/>$2I = \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi \\left( \\frac{\\cos^2 x}{1 + a^x} + \\frac{a^x \\cos^2 x}{a^x + 1} \\right) dx$<br/>$2I = \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi \\frac{\\cos^2 x (1 + a^x)}{1 + a^x} dx = \\int_{-\\pi}^\\pi \\cos^2 x dx$<br/>Since $\\cos^2 x$ is an even function, we can write:<br/>$2I = 2 \\int_0^\\pi \\cos^2 x dx \\implies I = \\int_0^\\pi \\cos^2 x dx$.<br/>Using the half-angle identity $\\cos^2 x = \\frac{1 + \\cos(2x)}{2}$:<br/>$I = \\int_0^\\pi \\frac{1 + \\cos(2x)}{2} dx = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ x + \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{2} \\right]_0^\\pi$<br/>$I = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\left(\\pi + \\frac{\\sin(2\\pi)}{2}\\right) - (0 + 0) \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} (\\pi + 0) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$.</p>",
