    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $J = \\int_3^{3+3T} f(2x) dx$.<br/>Substitute $u = 2x$, which implies $dx = \\frac{du}{2}$.<br/>When $x = 3, u = 6$. When $x = 3+3T, u = 6+6T$.<br/>$J = \\int_6^{6+6T} f(u) \\frac{du}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_6^{6+6T} f(u) du$.<br/>Since $f(x)$ is periodic with period $T$, any integral over an interval of length $nT$ is $n \\int_0^T f(x) dx$.<br/>The interval length here is $(6+6T) - 6 = 6T$, which is 6 times the period $T$.<br/>Therefore, $\\int_6^{6+6T} f(u) du = 6 \\int_0^T f(u) du = 6I$.<br/>$J = \\frac{1}{2} (6I) = 3I$.</p>",
