    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 3</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $\\int_{\\sin x}^1 t^2 f(t) dt = 1 - \\sin x$.<br/>Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$ using Leibniz\\'s rule:<br/>$0 - (\\sin x)^2 f(\\sin x) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x) = -\\cos x$.<br/>$-\\sin^2 x f(\\sin x) \\cos x = -\\cos x$.<br/>Since $x \\in (0, \\pi/2)$, $\\cos x > 0$, so we can divide by $-\\cos x$:<br/>$\\sin^2 x f(\\sin x) = 1 \\implies f(\\sin x) = \\frac{1}{\\sin^2 x}$.<br/>Let $u = \\sin x$. Then $f(u) = \\frac{1}{u^2}$.<br/>We need to evaluate $f\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)$:<br/>$f\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) = \\frac{1}{(1/\\sqrt{3})^2} = \\frac{1}{1/3} = 3$.</p>",
