    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: $\\frac{\\pi^2}{2} - 4$</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $I = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\left( x^2 + \\ln\\frac{\\pi+x}{\\pi-x} \\right) \\cos x dx$.<br/>We can split this into two integrals: $I = \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} x^2 \\cos x dx + \\int_{-\\pi/2}^{\\pi/2} \\ln\\frac{\\pi+x}{\\pi-x} \\cos x dx$.<br/>Consider $g(x) = \\ln\\frac{\\pi+x}{\\pi-x} \\cos x$.<br/>$g(-x) = \\ln\\frac{\\pi-x}{\\pi+x} \\cos(-x) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{\\pi+x}{\\pi-x}\\right)^{-1} \\cos x = -\\ln\\frac{\\pi+x}{\\pi-x} \\cos x = -g(x)$.<br/>Since $g(x)$ is an odd function, its integral over $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$ is $0$.<br/>The first term $f(x) = x^2 \\cos x$ is an even function.<br/>$I = 2 \\int_0^{\\pi/2} x^2 \\cos x dx$.<br/>Integrate by parts. Let $u = x^2$, $dv = \\cos x dx \\implies du = 2x dx$, $v = \\sin x$.<br/>$I = 2 \\left( [x^2 \\sin x]_0^{\\pi/2} - \\int_0^{\\pi/2} 2x \\sin x dx \\right)$.<br/>Integrate by parts again for $\\int 2x \\sin x dx$: $u = 2x, dv = \\sin x dx \\implies du = 2 dx, v = -\\cos x$.<br/>$\\int_0^{\\pi/2} 2x \\sin x dx = [-2x \\cos x]_0^{\\pi/2} - \\int_0^{\\pi/2} -2\\cos x dx$<br/>$= (0 - 0) + 2[\\sin x]_0^{\\pi/2} = 2(1) = 2$.<br/>So, $I = 2 \\left( \\left(\\frac{\\pi^2}{4}(1) - 0\\right) - 2 \\right) = 2 \\left( \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} - 2 \\right) = \\frac{\\pi^2}{2} - 4$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
