    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: $2 \\int_0^{\\ln(\\sqrt{2}+1)} (e^u + e^{-u})^{16} du$</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $I = \\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2} (2\\csc x)^{17} dx$.<br/>Use the substitution $u = \\ln(\\csc x - \\cot x)$.<br/>Differentiating, $du = \\csc x dx$.<br/>When $x = \\pi/4$, $u = \\ln(\\sqrt{2} - 1) = -\\ln(\\sqrt{2} + 1)$.<br/>When $x = \\pi/2$, $u = \\ln(1 - 0) = 0$.<br/>From $u = \\ln(\\csc x - \\cot x)$, we have $e^u = \\csc x - \\cot x$.<br/>Taking the reciprocal: $e^{-u} = \\frac{1}{\\csc x - \\cot x} = \\csc x + \\cot x$.<br/>Adding these gives $e^u + e^{-u} = 2\\csc x$.<br/>So, the integral becomes:<br/>$I = \\int_{-\\ln(\\sqrt{2}+1)}^0 (2\\csc x)^{16} \\cdot (2\\csc x dx) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}$?<br/>Wait, $(2\\csc x)^{17} dx = (2\\csc x)^{16} (2\\csc x dx) = (e^u + e^{-u})^{16} \\cdot 2 du$.<br/>Thus, $I = \\int_{-\\ln(\\sqrt{2}+1)}^0 2(e^u + e^{-u})^{16} du$.<br/>Since the integrand $(e^u + e^{-u})^{16}$ is an even function of $u$, we can flip the limits by taking the negative of the interval and it remains the same:<br/>$I = \\int_0^{\\ln(\\sqrt{2}+1)} 2(e^u + e^{-u})^{16} du$.</p>",
