    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $f\\'(x) = \\frac{192x^3}{2 + \\sin^4(\\pi x)}$ and $f(1/2) = 0$.<br/>To find bounds for $f(x)$ on $[1/2, 1]$, first bound $f\\'(x)$:<br/>Since $0 \\le \\sin^4(\\pi x) \\le 1$, we have $2 \\le 2 + \\sin^4(\\pi x) \\le 3$.<br/>Therefore, $\\frac{192x^3}{3} \\le f\\'(x) \\le \\frac{192x^3}{2} \\implies 64x^3 \\le f\\'(x) \\le 96x^3$.<br/>Integrate from $1/2$ to $x$ (where $x \\in [1/2, 1]$):<br/>$\\int_{1/2}^x 64t^3 dt \\le f(x) - f(1/2) \\le \\int_{1/2}^x 96t^3 dt$.<br/>$[16t^4]_{1/2}^x \\le f(x) \\le [24t^4]_{1/2}^x$.<br/>$16x^4 - 1 \\le f(x) \\le 24x^4 - 1.5$.<br/>Now integrate $f(x)$ from $1/2$ to $1$ to find $m$ and $M$:<br/>Lower bound $m$: $\\int_{1/2}^1 (16x^4 - 1) dx = \\left[ \\frac{16}{5}x^5 - x \\right]_{1/2}^1 = \\left(\\frac{16}{5} - 1\\right) - \\left(\\frac{16}{5}\\frac{1}{32} - \\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{11}{5} - \\left(\\frac{1}{10} - \\frac{5}{10}\\right) = 2.2 - (-0.4) = 2.6$.<br/>Upper bound $M$: $\\int_{1/2}^1 (24x^4 - 1.5) dx = \\left[ \\frac{24}{5}x^5 - 1.5x \\right]_{1/2}^1 = \\left(4.8 - 1.5\\right) - \\left(4.8\\frac{1}{32} - 0.75\\right) = 3.3 - (0.15 - 0.75) = 3.3 - (-0.6) = 3.9$.<br/>So $m = 2.6$ and $M = 3.9$.</p>",
