    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: No option is correct</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $g(x) = \\int_{\\sin x}^{\\sin 2x} \\sin^{-1}(t) dt$.<br/>By Leibniz\\'s rule, we differentiate $g(x)$:<br/>$g\\'(x) = \\sin^{-1}(\\sin 2x) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin 2x) - \\sin^{-1}(\\sin x) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)$.<br/>$g\\'(x) = \\sin^{-1}(\\sin 2x) \\cdot 2\\cos 2x - \\sin^{-1}(\\sin x) \\cdot \\cos x$.<br/>For small values of $x$ (e.g., $x \\in (-\\pi/4, \\pi/4)$):<br/>$g\\'(x) = (2x)(2\\cos 2x) - (x)(\\cos x) = 4x\\cos 2x - x\\cos x = x(4\\cos 2x - \\cos x)$.<br/>None of the given options matches this derivative or properties derived from it.</p>",
