    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 2</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $I = \\int_1^{e^{37}} \\frac{\\pi \\sin(\\pi \\ln x)}{x} dx$.<br/>Substitute $u = \\pi \\ln x$.<br/>Differentiate to find $du$: $du = \\frac{\\pi}{x} dx$.<br/>Change the limits of integration:<br/>When $x = 1$, $u = \\pi \\ln 1 = 0$.<br/>When $x = e^{37}$, $u = \\pi \\ln(e^{37}) = 37\\pi$.<br/>The integral becomes:<br/>$I = \\int_0^{37\\pi} \\sin u du = \\left[ -\\cos u \\right]_0^{37\\pi}$.<br/>$I = -\\cos(37\\pi) - (-\\cos 0) = -(-1) + 1 = 2$.</p>",
