    "chapter": "Definite Integrals",
    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 16</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $\\frac{d}{dx} F(x) = \\frac{e^{\\sin x}}{x}$, which means $F\\'(x) = \\frac{e^{\\sin x}}{x}$.<br/>We need to evaluate $I = \\int_1^4 \\frac{2 e^{\\sin x^2}}{x} dx$.<br/>Use the substitution $t = x^2$. Then $dt = 2x dx$, or $dx = \\frac{dt}{2x}$.<br/>The term $\\frac{2 dx}{x} = \\frac{2 (dt / 2x)}{x} = \\frac{dt}{x^2} = \\frac{dt}{t}$.<br/>Change the limits: when $x = 1, t = 1$; when $x = 4, t = 16$.<br/>The integral becomes:<br/>$I = \\int_1^{16} \\frac{e^{\\sin t}}{t} dt$.<br/>Notice that the integrand is exactly $F\\'(t)$.<br/>So, $I = \\int_1^{16} F\\'(t) dt = [F(t)]_1^{16} = F(16) - F(1)$.<br/>We are given $I = F(k) - F(1)$. Comparing the two expressions, we get $k = 16$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
