    "number": "90",
    "type": "fill_in_the_blanks",
    "text": "${lim}_{x\\rightarrow 0} \\frac{{∫}_{0}^{{x}^{2}} \\frac{cos{t}^{2}dt}{xsinx} =}{=}$ $$ _____________",
    "images": [],
    "options": [],
    "correct": "1",
    "chapter": "Definite Integrals",
    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 1</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We need to evaluate $L = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\int_0^{x^2} \\cos(t^2) dt}{x \\sin x}$.<br/>As $x \\to 0$, both the numerator $\\int_0^0 \\cos(t^2) dt = 0$ and the denominator $0 \\cdot 0 = 0$ approach 0. This is a $0/0$ indeterminate form.<br/>Apply L\\'Hopital\\'s Rule by differentiating the numerator and the denominator with respect to $x$.<br/>Derivative of the numerator using Leibniz\\'s Rule:<br/>$\\frac{d}{dx} \\left( \\int_0^{x^2} \\cos(t^2) dt \\right) = \\cos((x^2)^2) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^2) = 2x \\cos(x^4)$.<br/>Derivative of the denominator using the product rule:<br/>$\\frac{d}{dx}(x \\sin x) = \\sin x + x \\cos x$.<br/>Now the limit becomes:<br/>$L = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2x \\cos(x^4)}{\\sin x + x \\cos x}$.<br/>Divide the numerator and the denominator by $x$:<br/>$L = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2 \\cos(x^4)}{\\frac{\\sin x}{x} + \\cos x}$.<br/>As $x \\to 0$, $\\cos(x^4) \\to 1$, $\\frac{\\sin x}{x} \\to 1$, and $\\cos x \\to 1$.<br/>$L = \\frac{2(1)}{1 + 1} = \\frac{2}{2} = 1$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Easy"
