    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $3 \\int_1^x f(t) dt = x f(x) - \\frac{x^3}{3}$.<br/>Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$ using Leibniz\\'s rule:<br/>$3 f(x) = f(x) + x f\\'(x) - x^2$.<br/>$x f\\'(x) - 2f(x) = x^2 \\implies f\\'(x) - \\frac{2}{x} f(x) = x$.<br/>This is a linear differential equation. The integrating factor (IF) is:<br/>$IF = e^{\\int -\\frac{2}{x} dx} = e^{-2\\ln x} = x^{-2} = \\frac{1}{x^2}$.<br/>Multiply the equation by the IF:<br/>$\\frac{d}{dx} \\left( f(x) \\cdot \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right) = x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x^2} = \\frac{1}{x}$.<br/>Integrate both sides:<br/>$\\frac{f(x)}{x^2} = \\int \\frac{1}{x} dx = \\ln x + C \\implies f(x) = x^2 \\ln x + C x^2$.<br/>We are given $f(1) = 1/3$:<br/>$\\frac{1}{3} = 1^2 \\ln 1 + C(1^2) = C \\implies C = \\frac{1}{3}$.<br/>So $f(x) = x^2 \\ln x + \\frac{x^2}{3}$.<br/>Find $f(e)$:<br/>$f(e) = e^2 \\ln e + \\frac{e^2}{3} = e^2 + \\frac{e^2}{3} = \\frac{4e^2}{3}$.</p>",
