    "correct": "\\frac{13x",
    "chapter": "Differential Equations",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $\\lim_{t \\to x} \\frac{t^{10} f(x) - x^{10} f(t)}{t^9 - x^9} = 1$.<br/>This is a 0/0 form. Apply L\\'Hopital\\'s rule by differentiating the numerator and denominator with respect to $t$:<br/>$\\lim_{t \\to x} \\frac{10t^9 f(x) - x^{10} f\\'(t)}{9t^8} = \\frac{10x^9 f(x) - x^{10} f\\'(x)}{9x^8} = \\frac{10x f(x) - x^2 f\\'(x)}{9} = 1$.<br/>Rearrange the equation:<br/>$x^2 f\\'(x) - 10x f(x) = -9 \\implies f\\'(x) - \\frac{10}{x} f(x) = -\\frac{9}{x^2}$.<br/>This is a linear differential equation. The integrating factor is:<br/>$IF = e^{\\int -\\frac{10}{x} dx} = x^{-10}$.<br/>Multiply by the IF:<br/>$\\frac{d}{dx} (f(x) x^{-10}) = -9 x^{-12}$.<br/>Integrate:<br/>$f(x) x^{-10} = \\frac{-9 x^{-11}}{-11} + C = \\frac{9}{11 x^{11}} + C$.<br/>$f(x) = \\frac{9}{11 x} + C x^{10}$.<br/>Given $f(1) = 2$:<br/>$2 = \\frac{9}{11} + C \\implies C = 2 - \\frac{9}{11} = \\frac{13}{11}$.<br/>Therefore, $f(x) = \\frac{9}{11 x} + \\frac{13}{11} x^{10} = \\frac{13x^{11} + 9}{11x}$.</p>",
