    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 0</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The given equation is $y\\'(x) + g\\'(x) y(x) = g(x) g\\'(x)$.<br/>This is a linear differential equation in $y$. The integrating factor is:<br/>$IF = e^{\\int g\\'(x) dx} = e^{g(x)}$.<br/>Multiply by the IF:<br/>$\\frac{d}{dx} \\left( y e^{g(x)} \\right) = g(x) g\\'(x) e^{g(x)}$.<br/>Integrate both sides:<br/>$y e^{g(x)} = \\int g(x) e^{g(x)} g\\'(x) dx$.<br/>Let $u = g(x)$, then $du = g\\'(x) dx$. The integral becomes:<br/>$\\int u e^u du = u e^u - e^u + C = e^u(u - 1) + C$.<br/>Substituting back $u = g(x)$:<br/>$y e^{g(x)} = e^{g(x)}(g(x) - 1) + C \\implies y(x) = g(x) - 1 + C e^{-g(x)}$.<br/>Given $y(0) = 0$ and $g(0) = 0$:<br/>$0 = 0 - 1 + C e^0 \\implies 0 = -1 + C \\implies C = 1$.<br/>So $y(x) = g(x) - 1 + e^{-g(x)}$.<br/>We need to find $y(2)$. Given $g(2) = 0$:<br/>$y(2) = g(2) - 1 + e^{-g(2)} = 0 - 1 + e^0 = -1 + 1 = 0$.</p>",
