    "correct": "2",
    "chapter": "Differential Equations",
    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 2</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given the functional equation $f(x+y) = f(x)f\\'(y) + f\\'(x)f(y)$ and $f(0)=1$.<br/>Substitute $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:<br/>$f(0) = f(0)f\\'(0) + f\\'(0)f(0) = 2f(0)f\\'(0)$.<br/>Since $f(0) = 1$, we have $1 = 2(1)f\\'(0) \\implies f\\'(0) = \\frac{1}{2}$.<br/>Now substitute $y=0$ in the original equation:<br/>$f(x) = f(x)f\\'(0) + f\\'(x)f(0)$.<br/>Using $f(0)=1$ and $f\\'(0)=1/2$:<br/>$f(x) = \\frac{1}{2} f(x) + f\\'(x) \\implies f\\'(x) = \\frac{1}{2} f(x)$.<br/>This is a separable differential equation: $\\frac{f\\'(x)}{f(x)} = \\frac{1}{2}$.<br/>Integrate both sides:<br/>$\\ln|f(x)| = \\frac{1}{2}x + C$.<br/>Given $f(0) = 1$, we have $\\ln(1) = 0 + C \\implies C = 0$.<br/>So $\\ln f(x) = \\frac{x}{2} \\implies f(x) = e^{x/2}$.<br/>We need to find $\\ln(f(4))$:<br/>$\\ln(f(4)) = \\ln(e^{4/2}) = \\ln(e^2) = 2$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
