    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 8</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The given differential equation is $x dy - (y^2 - 4y) dx = 0$.<br/>Separate variables:<br/>$\\frac{dy}{y^2 - 4y} = \\frac{dx}{x}$.<br/>Integrate both sides:<br/>$\\int \\frac{dy}{y(y-4)} = \\int \\frac{dx}{x}$.<br/>Use partial fractions for the left side: $\\frac{1}{y(y-4)} = \\frac{1}{4}\\left( \\frac{1}{y-4} - \\frac{1}{y} \\right)$.<br/>$\\frac{1}{4} \\int \\left( \\frac{1}{y-4} - \\frac{1}{y} \\right) dy = \\ln|x| + C$.<br/>$\\frac{1}{4} (\\ln|y-4| - \\ln|y|) = \\ln x + C \\implies \\ln\\left| \\frac{y-4}{y} \\right| = 4\\ln x + 4C = \\ln(x^4) + C\\'$.<br/>Given $y(1) = 2$:<br/>$\\ln\\left| \\frac{2-4}{2} \\right| = \\ln(1^4) + C\\' \\implies \\ln|-1| = 0 + C\\' \\implies C\\' = \\ln 1 = 0$.<br/>So $\\left| \\frac{y-4}{y} \\right| = x^4$.<br/>Since $\\frac{y-4}{y}$ is negative at $x=1$ (it is -1), we drop the absolute value with a negative sign:<br/>$\\frac{y-4}{y} = -x^4 \\implies 1 - \\frac{4}{y} = -x^4 \\implies \\frac{4}{y} = 1 + x^4 \\implies y = \\frac{4}{1+x^4}$.<br/>We need to find $10 y(\\sqrt{2})$:<br/>At $x = \\sqrt{2}$, $x^4 = (\\sqrt{2})^4 = 4$.<br/>$y(\\sqrt{2}) = \\frac{4}{1+4} = \\frac{4}{5} = 0.8$.<br/>Then $10 y(\\sqrt{2}) = 10(0.8) = 8$.</p>",
