    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $y = (\\sin x)^{\\tan x}$.<br/>Take the natural logarithm of both sides:<br/>$\\ln y = \\tan x \\ln(\\sin x)$.<br/>Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$ using the product rule:<br/>$\\frac{1}{y} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{d}{dx}(\\tan x) \\cdot \\ln(\\sin x) + \\tan x \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln(\\sin x))$.<br/>$\\frac{1}{y} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sec^2 x \\ln(\\sin x) + \\tan x \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sin x} \\cos x$.<br/>Since $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$, the second term simplifies to $\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x} = 1$.<br/>So, $\\frac{1}{y} \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sec^2 x \\ln(\\sin x) + 1$.<br/>$\\frac{dy}{dx} = y (1 + \\sec^2 x \\ln(\\sin x)) = (\\sin x)^{\\tan x} (1 + \\sec^2 x \\ln(\\sin x))$.<br/>This matches option (a).</p>",
