    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $\\log(x+y) - 2xy = 0$. Assuming natural logarithm $\\ln(x+y) - 2xy = 0$.<br/>First, find the value of $y$ at $x = 0$:<br/>$\\ln(0 + y) - 2(0)(y) = 0 \\implies \\ln y = 0 \\implies y = 1$.<br/>So the point is $(0, 1)$.<br/>Differentiate implicitly with respect to $x$:<br/>$\\frac{1}{x+y} \\left( 1 + \\frac{dy}{dx} \\right) - 2 \\left( y + x\\frac{dy}{dx} \\right) = 0$.<br/>Substitute $x = 0$ and $y = 1$:<br/>$\\frac{1}{0+1} \\left( 1 + y\\'(0) \\right) - 2(1 + 0 \\cdot y\\'(0)) = 0$.<br/>$1(1 + y\\'(0)) - 2(1) = 0 \\implies 1 + y\\'(0) - 2 = 0 \\implies y\\'(0) - 1 = 0 \\implies y\\'(0) = 1$.<br/>The value is 1, which corresponds to option (a).</p>",
