    "id": "jee_adv_math_differentiation_24",
    "number": "24",
    "type": "fill_in_the_blanks",
    "text": "If $x{e}^{xy}=y+{sin}^{2}x$ , then at $x=0, \\frac{dy}{dx} =$ ___",
    "images": [],
    "options": [],
    "correct": "1",
    "chapter": "Differentiation",
    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 1</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $x e^{xy} = y + \\sin^2 x$.<br/>Find the value of $y$ at $x = 0$:<br/>$0 \\cdot e^0 = y(0) + \\sin^2(0) \\implies 0 = y(0) + 0 \\implies y(0) = 0$.<br/>Differentiate the equation implicitly with respect to $x$:<br/>$1 \\cdot e^{xy} + x \\cdot e^{xy} \\cdot (y + x y\\') = y\\' + 2\\sin x \\cos x$.<br/>Substitute $x = 0$ and $y = 0$:<br/>$e^0 + 0 \\cdot e^0 \\cdot (0 + 0) = y\\'(0) + 2\\sin 0 \\cos 0$.<br/>$1 + 0 = y\\'(0) + 0 \\implies y\\'(0) = 1$.<br/>So, $\\frac{dy}{dx} = 1$ at $x = 0$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
