    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The ellipse is $\\frac{x^2}{16} + \\frac{y^2}{4} = 1$, where $a=4, b=2, e = \\sqrt{1 - 4/16} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<br/>The normal at $P(4\\cos\\theta, 2\\sin\\theta)$ is given by $\\frac{a^2 x}{x_1} - \\frac{b^2 y}{y_1} = a^2 - b^2$:<br/>$\\frac{16x}{4\\cos\\theta} - \\frac{4y}{2\\sin\\theta} = 16 - 4 \\implies \\frac{4x}{\\cos\\theta} - \\frac{2y}{\\sin\\theta} = 12 \\implies \\frac{2x}{\\cos\\theta} - \\frac{y}{\\sin\\theta} = 6$.<br/>The normal meets the x-axis at $Q$: put $y = 0$, then $x = 3\\cos\\theta$. So $Q = (3\\cos\\theta, 0)$.<br/>$M$ is the midpoint of $P(4\\cos\\theta, 2\\sin\\theta)$ and $Q(3\\cos\\theta, 0)$:<br/>$M = \\left(\\frac{4\\cos\\theta + 3\\cos\\theta}{2}, \\frac{2\\sin\\theta + 0}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\cos\\theta, \\sin\\theta\\right)$.<br/>Let $M = (x,y)$. Then $x = \\frac{7}{2}\\cos\\theta$ and $y = \\sin\\theta$.<br/>Locus of $M$: $\\frac{x^2}{(7/2)^2} + y^2 = 1 \\implies \\frac{4x^2}{49} + y^2 = 1$.<br/>The latus rectum of the given ellipse is $x = \\pm ae = \\pm 4\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}$.<br/>Substitute $x = \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}$ into the locus of $M$:<br/>$\\frac{4(12)}{49} + y^2 = 1 \\implies y^2 = 1 - \\frac{48}{49} = \\frac{1}{49} \\implies y = \\pm \\frac{1}{7}$.<br/>The intersection points are $(\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}, \\pm \\frac{1}{7})$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
