    "chapter": "Ellipse",
    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b, c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The ellipse is $x^2 + 4y^2 = 4 \\implies \\frac{x^2}{4} + \\frac{y^2}{1} = 1$.<br/>Here $a^2 = 4, b^2 = 1$. The eccentricity is $e = \\sqrt{1 - 1/4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<br/>The foci are $(\\pm ae, 0) = (\\pm \\sqrt{3}, 0)$.<br/>The endpoints of the latus rectum have $x = \\pm \\sqrt{3}$ and $y = \\pm \\frac{b^2}{a} = \\pm \\frac{1}{2}$.<br/>We are given $P(x_1, y_1)$ and $Q(x_2, y_2)$ with $y_1 < 0$ and $y_2 < 0$.<br/>Thus, $P = (\\sqrt{3}, -1/2)$ and $Q = (-\\sqrt{3}, -1/2)$.<br/>The line segment $PQ$ is horizontal with length $2\\sqrt{3}$ and lies on $y = -1/2$.<br/>We need the equations of parabolas with latus rectum $PQ$.<br/>The length of the latus rectum is $4a\\' = 2\\sqrt{3} \\implies a\\' = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<br/>The focus of the parabola is the midpoint of $PQ$, which is $(0, -1/2)$.<br/>The axis of the parabola is the perpendicular bisector of $PQ$, which is the y-axis.<br/>The vertex of the parabola is at a distance $a\\' = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ from the focus along the y-axis.<br/>Vertex $V = (0, -1/2 \\pm \\sqrt{3}/2)$.<br/>If the parabola opens upward, its equation is $x^2 = 2\\sqrt{3}\\left(y - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) = 2\\sqrt{3}y + \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\implies x^2 - 2\\sqrt{3}y = 3 + \\sqrt{3}$.<br/>If the parabola opens downward, its equation is $x^2 = -2\\sqrt{3}\\left(y - \\left(-\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\right) = -2\\sqrt{3}y - \\sqrt{3} + 3 \\implies x^2 + 2\\sqrt{3}y = 3 - \\sqrt{3}$.</p>",
