    "correct": "c",
    "chapter": "Function",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions\n (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = |px - q| + r|x|$ with $p>0, q>0, r>0$.<br/>The critical points of this piecewise linear function are where the arguments of the absolute values are zero: $x = 0$ and $x = q/p$.<br/>Between these critical points, for $0 < x < q/p$:<br/>$px - q < 0$ and $x > 0$, so:<br/>$f(x) = -(px - q) + rx = (r - p)x + q$.<br/>This is a line segment with slope $r - p$.<br/>If $r = p$, the slope is $0$, which means $f(x)$ is constant and equal to $q$ on the entire interval $[0, q/p]$. In this case, the minimum value is assumed at infinitely many points.<br/>If $r \\neq p$, the slope is non-zero, meaning the minimum must occur uniquely at one of the endpoints of the intervals (i.e., at exactly one point, either $0$ or $q/p$).<br/>So, the function assumes its minimum value only at one point if $r \\neq p$.</p>",
