    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We have $f(x) = \\frac{x^2 - 1}{x^2 + 1}$.<br/>We can rewrite this as:<br/>$f(x) = \\frac{x^2 + 1 - 2}{x^2 + 1} = 1 - \\frac{2}{x^2 + 1}$.<br/>To find the minimum value of $f(x)$, we need to maximize the term being subtracted, which is $\\frac{2}{x^2 + 1}$.<br/>Since $x^2 \\ge 0$ for all real $x$, $x^2 + 1 \\ge 1$.<br/>The maximum value of $\\frac{2}{x^2 + 1}$ occurs when the denominator is minimized, which is at $x = 0$.<br/>At $x = 0$, $\\frac{2}{0 + 1} = 2$.<br/>So the minimum value of $f(x)$ is $1 - 2 = -1$.<br/>This minimum is attained at $x = 0$, so it is equal to -1.</p>",
