    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions\n (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The function is $f(x) = \\cos x + \\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)$.<br/>The maximum value of both $\\cos x$ and $\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x)$ is $1$.<br/>Thus, the maximum possible value of $f(x)$ is $1 + 1 = 2$.<br/>This maximum is attained only when both terms are simultaneously equal to $1$.<br/>$\\cos x = 1 \\implies x = 2m\\pi$ for some integer $m$.<br/>$\\cos(\\sqrt{2}x) = 1 \\implies \\sqrt{2}x = 2n\\pi$ for some integer $n$.<br/>Equating the two expressions for $x$:<br/>$2m\\pi = \\sqrt{2}n\\pi \\implies \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{m}{n}$.<br/>Since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational, this equation can only be satisfied if $m = 0$ and $n = 0$.<br/>This gives exactly one solution: $x = 0$.<br/>So the function attains its maximum at exactly $1$ point.</p>",
