    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: (-\\infty, 1)</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given the equation $2^x + 2^y = 2$.<br/>We can express $y$ in terms of $x$ as:<br/>$2^y = 2 - 2^x$.<br/>For $y$ to be a real number, $2^y$ must be strictly positive.<br/>Therefore, we must have:<br/>$2 - 2^x > 0 \\implies 2^x < 2 \\implies 2^x < 2^1$.<br/>Since the exponential function with base $2$ is strictly increasing, this implies $x < 1$.<br/>Thus, the domain of definition of the function $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty, 1)$.<br/>(This matches the option likely labeled as d).</p>",
