    "chapter": "Function",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions\n (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let the sets be $E = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ and $F = \\{1, 2\\}$.<br/>The total number of functions from a set with $m$ elements to a set with $n$ elements is $n^m$.<br/>Here, total functions from $E$ to $F$ is $2^4 = 16$.<br/>An onto (surjective) function from $E$ to $F$ must map to both $1$ and $2$.<br/>The functions that are not onto are those that map every element of $E$ to just one element of $F$.<br/>There are exactly $2$ such functions: one that maps everything to $1$, and one that maps everything to $2$.<br/>Number of onto functions $=$ Total functions $-$ Non-onto functions $= 16 - 2 = 14$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Easy"
