    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions\n (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = \\frac{\\alpha x}{x+1}$.<br/>We are given that $f(f(x)) = x$ for all $x \\neq -1$.<br/>Substitute $f(x)$ into itself:<br/>$f(f(x)) = \\frac{\\alpha f(x)}{f(x) + 1} = \\frac{\\alpha \\left(\\frac{\\alpha x}{x+1}\\right)}{\\frac{\\alpha x}{x+1} + 1}$.<br/>Simplify the numerator and denominator by multiplying by $(x+1)$:<br/>$f(f(x)) = \\frac{\\alpha^2 x}{\\alpha x + (x + 1)} = \\frac{\\alpha^2 x}{(\\alpha + 1)x + 1}$.<br/>We want this to equal $x$:<br/>$\\frac{\\alpha^2 x}{(\\alpha + 1)x + 1} = x$.<br/>Since this holds for all valid $x$, we can equate coefficients:<br/>$\\alpha^2 x = (\\alpha + 1)x^2 + x$.<br/>For the coefficients of $x^2$ to match, we must have $\\alpha + 1 = 0 \\implies \\alpha = -1$.<br/>For the coefficients of $x$ to match, we must have $\\alpha^2 = 1$, which is consistent with $\\alpha = -1$.<br/>Thus, $\\alpha = -1$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
