    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions\n (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = 2x + \\sin x$.<br/>To check if it is one-to-one, we find its derivative: $f\\'(x) = 2 + \\cos x$.<br/>Since $-1 \\le \\cos x \\le 1$ for all real $x$, we have $2 - 1 \\le f\\'(x) \\le 2 + 1 \\implies 1 \\le f\\'(x) \\le 3$.<br/>Since $f\\'(x) > 0$ for all real $x$, $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, which means it is one-to-one (injective).<br/>To check if it is onto, we find the limits as $x \\to \\pm \\infty$:<br/>As $x \\to \\infty$, $f(x) \\to \\infty$.<br/>As $x \\to -\\infty$, $f(x) \\to -\\infty$.<br/>Since $f(x)$ is continuous and its limits at $\\pm \\infty$ span the entire real line, its range is all real numbers ($\\mathbb{R}$).<br/>Therefore, $f(x)$ is onto (surjective).<br/>Hence, $f(x)$ is one-to-one and onto.</p>",
