    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: \\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\{-1, 1\\}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let\\'s analyze the continuity of $f(x)$ at the critical points $x = 1$ and $x = -1$.<br/>At $x = 1$:<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} f(x) = \\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$.<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 1^+} f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}(|1| - 1) = 0$.<br/>Since the left and right limits are not equal, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x = 1$. Thus, it is not differentiable at $x = 1$.<br/>At $x = -1$:<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to -1^+} f(x) = \\tan^{-1}(-1) = -\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to -1^-} f(x) = \\frac{1}{2}(|-1| - 1) = 0$.<br/>Again, the left and right limits are not equal, so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x = -1$. Thus, it is not differentiable at $x = -1$.<br/>For all other points ($|x| < 1$ and $|x| > 1$), the pieces are standard differentiable functions (inverse tangent and linear functions).<br/>Therefore, the domain of the derivative is all real numbers except $-1$ and $1$, which is $\\mathbb{R} \\setminus \\{-1, 1\\}$.<br/>(This matches option d).</p>",
