    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f: [0, \\infty) \\to [0, \\infty)$ with $f(x) = \\frac{x}{1+x}$.<br/>To check for one-one (injectivity), find the derivative:<br/>$f\\'(x) = \\frac{1 \\cdot (1+x) - x \\cdot 1}{(1+x)^2} = \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}$.<br/>Since $f\\'(x) > 0$ for all $x \\ge 0$, the function is strictly increasing, hence it is one-one.<br/>To check for onto (surjectivity), find the range.<br/>At $x = 0$, $f(0) = 0$.<br/>As $x \\to \\infty$, $\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{x}{1+x} = \\lim_{x \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{1/x + 1} = 1$.<br/>So the range of $f$ is $[0, 1)$.<br/>Since the codomain is given as $[0, \\infty)$ and the range is $[0, 1) \\neq [0, \\infty)$, the function is not onto.<br/>Thus, it is one-one but not onto.</p>",
