    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: (1, 7/3]</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $y = \\frac{x^2 + x + 2}{x^2 + x + 1}$.<br/>We can rewrite this as $y = \\frac{(x^2 + x + 1) + 1}{x^2 + x + 1} = 1 + \\frac{1}{x^2 + x + 1}$.<br/>Let\\'s find the range of the denominator $D = x^2 + x + 1$.<br/>Completing the square: $D = \\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 + \\frac{3}{4}$.<br/>Since $\\left(x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 \\ge 0$, the minimum value of $D$ is $\\frac{3}{4}$. The maximum value is $\\infty$.<br/>So the range of $D$ is $[3/4, \\infty)$.<br/>Then the range of $\\frac{1}{D}$ is $(0, \\frac{4}{3}]$.<br/>Finally, the range of $y = 1 + \\frac{1}{D}$ is $(1, 1 + \\frac{4}{3}] = \\left(1, \\frac{7}{3}\\right]$.</p>",
