    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The question asks for true statements about pre-images and images of subsets under a function $f: X \\to Y$.<br/>Let $A \\subseteq X$ and $B \\subseteq Y$.<br/>The image of $A$ is $f(A) = \\{f(x) : x \\in A\\}$.<br/>The pre-image of $B$ is $f^{-1}(B) = \\{x \\in X : f(x) \\in B\\}$.<br/>Two standard set-theoretic results for functions are:<br/>1. $A \\subseteq f^{-1}(f(A))$ for any subset $A \\subseteq X$. (Equality holds if $f$ is injective).<br/>2. $f(f^{-1}(B)) \\subseteq B$ for any subset $B \\subseteq Y$. (Equality holds if $f$ is surjective).<br/>Option c corresponds to these inclusion properties (despite the parsing corruption in the text). Therefore, the correct statement is that $A \\subseteq f^{-1}(f(A))$ and $f(f^{-1}(B)) \\subseteq B$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
