    "chapter": "Function",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions\n (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $F(x) = \\left(f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\right)^2 + \\left(g\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\right)^2$, where $f\\'\\'(x) = -f(x)$ and $g(x) = f\\'(x)$.<br/>Let\\'s find the derivative of $F(x)$ with respect to $x$:<br/>$F\\'(x) = 2 f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) f\\'\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} + 2 g\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) g\\'\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}$<br/>$F\\'(x) = f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) f\\'\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + g\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) g\\'\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$.<br/>From the given conditions, $f\\'(x) = g(x)$ and $g\\'(x) = f\\'\\'(x) = -f(x)$.<br/>Substituting these into the expression for $F\\'(x)$:<br/>$F\\'(x) = f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) g\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + g\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) \\left(-f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\right)$<br/>$F\\'(x) = f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) g\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) - f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) g\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) = 0$.<br/>Since $F\\'(x) = 0$ for all $x$, $F(x)$ is a constant function.<br/>We are given that $F(5) = 5$. Since $F(x)$ is constant, $F(x) = 5$ for all $x$.<br/>Therefore, $F(10) = 5$, which matches option a.</p>",
