    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a, c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = \\cos([\\pi^2]x) + \\cos([-\\pi^2]x)$.<br/>We know $\\pi \\approx 3.14159$, so $\\pi^2 \\approx 9.8696$.<br/>The greatest integer less than or equal to $\\pi^2$ is $[\\pi^2] = 9$.<br/>The greatest integer less than or equal to $-\\pi^2$ is $[-\\pi^2] = -10$.<br/>Substitute these into the function:<br/>$f(x) = \\cos(9x) + \\cos(-10x) = \\cos(9x) + \\cos(10x)$.<br/>Now let\\'s test the options:<br/>(a) $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{9\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\cos\\left(\\frac{10\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos\\left(4\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\cos(5\\pi) = 0 + (-1) = -1$. (True)<br/>(b) $f(\\pi) = \\cos(9\\pi) + \\cos(10\\pi) = -1 + 1 = 0$. (False, option says 1)<br/>(c) $f(-\\pi) = \\cos(-9\\pi) + \\cos(-10\\pi) = \\cos(9\\pi) + \\cos(10\\pi) = -1 + 1 = 0$. (True)<br/>(d) $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{9\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\cos\\left(\\frac{10\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\cos\\left(2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\cos\\left(2\\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} + 0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$. (False, option says 1)<br/>Therefore, options a and c are correct.</p>",
