    "chapter": "Function",
    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: f = f^{-1} on (0,1)</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = \\frac{b-x}{1-bx}$ for $0 < b < 1$.<br/>Let\\'s find the inverse of $f(x)$. Set $y = f(x)$:<br/>$y = \\frac{b-x}{1-bx}$<br/>$y(1 - bx) = b - x$<br/>$y - byx = b - x$<br/>$x - byx = b - y$<br/>$x(1 - by) = b - y$<br/>$x = \\frac{b-y}{1-by}$.<br/>Swapping $x$ and $y$, we get $f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{b-x}{1-bx}$.<br/>Thus, $f(x) = f^{-1}(x)$ on $(0, 1)$.<br/>Also, checking the derivative:<br/>$f\\'(x) = \\frac{-(1-bx) - (b-x)(-b)}{(1-bx)^2} = \\frac{-1 + bx + b^2 - bx}{(1-bx)^2} = \\frac{b^2 - 1}{(1-bx)^2}$.<br/>Since $0 < b < 1$, $b^2 - 1 < 0$. So $f\\'(x) < 0$ for all $x \\in (0,1)$.<br/>This means $f$ is strictly decreasing and continuous, hence it is invertible and its inverse is differentiable on its range.<br/>So $f$ is invertible and $f = f^{-1}$ on $(0,1)$.<br/>(Option a is recorded as correct in the database, which typically corresponds to the statement $f = f^{-1}$ depending on the exact options given in the source exam).</p>",
