    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 1 \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(\\cos 4\\theta) = \\frac{2}{2 - \\sec^2\\theta}$.<br/>Simplify the RHS:<br/>$\\frac{2}{2 - \\frac{1}{\\cos^2\\theta}} = \\frac{2}{\\frac{2\\cos^2\\theta - 1}{\\cos^2\\theta}} = \\frac{2\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\frac{1 + \\cos 2\\theta}{\\cos 2\\theta} = \\frac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} + 1$.<br/>So, $f(\\cos 4\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\cos 2\\theta} + 1$.<br/>Let $x = \\cos 4\\theta$. Then $\\cos 4\\theta = 2\\cos^2 2\\theta - 1 = x$.<br/>$2\\cos^2 2\\theta = x + 1 \\implies \\cos^2 2\\theta = \\frac{x+1}{2}$.<br/>$\\cos 2\\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{2}}$.<br/>The sign depends on the interval of $\\theta$:<br/>If $\\theta \\in (0, \\pi/4)$, then $2\\theta \\in (0, \\pi/2)$, so $\\cos 2\\theta > 0$.<br/>If $\\theta \\in (\\pi/4, \\pi/2)$, then $2\\theta \\in (\\pi/2, \\pi)$, so $\\cos 2\\theta < 0$.<br/>We want to evaluate $f(1/3)$, so $x = 1/3$.<br/>$\\cos^2 2\\theta = \\frac{1/3 + 1}{2} = \\frac{4/3}{2} = \\frac{2}{3}$.<br/>So $\\cos 2\\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$.<br/>Then $f(1/3) = \\frac{1}{\\pm \\sqrt{2/3}} + 1 = 1 \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}$.</p>",
