    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a, b, c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let\\'s evaluate the given functions.<br/>For $f(x) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6} \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\sin x\\right)\\right)$:<br/>Since $\\sin x \\in [-1, 1]$, we have $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin x \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$.<br/>Then $\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin x\\right) \\in [-1, 1]$.<br/>Then $\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin x\\right) \\in \\left[-\\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right]$.<br/>Taking the sine again, the range is $\\left[\\sin\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right), \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\right] = \\left[-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right]$. So (a) is true.<br/>For (b), $f(g(x)) = f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin x\\right)$. Since $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin x$ also spans $[-\\pi/2, \\pi/2]$, the inner expression evaluates the same range, so the range of $f \\circ g$ is also $\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right]$. (b) is true.<br/>For (c), let $u = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin x$. As $x \\to 0$, $u \\to 0$.<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \\lim_{u \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6}\\sin u\\right)}{u} = \\frac{\\pi}{6}$. So (c) is true.<br/>For (d), $(g \\circ f)(x) = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\sin(f(x))$. Since $f(x) \\in [-1/2, 1/2]$, $(g \\circ f)(x)$ is at most $\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\sin(1/2) \\approx 0.75 < 1$. Thus it can never equal 1. (d) is false.<br/>Therefore, a, b, and c are correct.</p>",
