    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: Check Answer Key</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $f(x) = x^3 + 3x + 2$.<br/>We are also given $g(f(x)) = x$, which implies $g$ is the inverse function of $f$, so $g(x) = f^{-1}(x)$.<br/>Furthermore, $h(g(g(x))) = x$. Substitute $y = g(g(x))$, then $x = f(f(y))$.<br/>Therefore, $h(y) = f(f(y))$, or $h(x) = f(f(x))$.<br/>This allows us to evaluate $h(x)$ or its derivatives at any point.<br/>For example, $h(0) = f(f(0)) = f(2) = 2^3 + 3(2) + 2 = 16$.<br/>If the question asked for $h\\'(x)$, we can find it using the chain rule: $h\\'(x) = f\\'(f(x))f\\'(x)$.<br/>(Note: the provided answer key \\\"43.\\\" seems to be a parsing error from the original question document.)</p>",
