    "chapter": "Function",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: [-1, 2) \\cup [3, \\infty)</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>For $y$ to take real values, the expression inside the square root must be non-negative:<br/>$\\frac{(x+1)(x-3)}{x-2} \\ge 0$.<br/>The critical points where the numerator or denominator are zero are $x = -1, 2, 3$.<br/>We plot these on a number line to check the sign of the expression in each interval:<br/>1. For $x > 3$, e.g., $x = 4$: $(+)(+) / (+) > 0$.<br/>2. For $2 < x < 3$, e.g., $x = 2.5$: $(+)(-) / (+) < 0$.<br/>3. For $-1 < x < 2$, e.g., $x = 0$: $(+)(-) / (-) > 0$.<br/>4. For $x < -1$, e.g., $x = -2$: (-)(-) / (-) < 0.<br/>The expression is non-negative on $[-1, 2)$ and $[3, \\infty)$.<br/>Note that $x=2$ is excluded because it makes the denominator zero.<br/>So the real values of $x$ are $x \\in [-1, 2) \\cup [3, \\infty)$.</p>",
