    "topic": "True/False",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: True</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>By the definition of operations on functions, the sum $(f_1 + f_2)(x) = f_1(x) + f_2(x)$.<br/>For this expression to be defined, both $f_1(x)$ and $f_2(x)$ must be defined simultaneously.<br/>$f_1(x)$ is defined for $x \\in D_1$ and $f_2(x)$ is defined for $x \\in D_2$.<br/>Both are defined when $x \\in D_1$ and $x \\in D_2$, which means $x \\in D_1 \\cap D_2$.<br/>Thus, the domain of $f_1 + f_2$ is $D_1 \\cap D_2$.<br/>The statement is true.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
