    "chapter": "Function",
    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: [0, 3/\\sqrt{2}]</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = 3\\sin\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi^2}{16} - x^2}\\right)$.<br/>For the function to be defined, the expression inside the square root must be non-negative:<br/>$\\frac{\\pi^2}{16} - x^2 \\ge 0 \\implies 0 \\le \\frac{\\pi^2}{16} - x^2 \\le \\frac{\\pi^2}{16}$.<br/>Taking the square root, the value of the argument to the sine function ranges as follows:<br/>$0 \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi^2}{16} - x^2} \\le \\frac{\\pi}{4}$.<br/>The sine function is strictly increasing on the interval $[0, \\pi/4]$.<br/>Thus, $\\sin(0) \\le \\sin\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi^2}{16} - x^2}\\right) \\le \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$.<br/>$0 \\le \\sin\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi^2}{16} - x^2}\\right) \\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$.<br/>Multiplying by $3$, the values of $f(x)$ lie in the interval $\\left[0, \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right]$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
