    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: [-2, -1] \\cup [1, 2]</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\log_2 \\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)$.<br/>The domain of the inverse sine function is $[-1, 1]$, so we must have:<br/>$-1 \\le \\log_2 \\left(\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right) \\le 1$.<br/>Since the base of the logarithm is $2 > 1$, we can exponentiate to get:<br/>$2^{-1} \\le \\frac{x^2}{2} \\le 2^1$<br/>$\\frac{1}{2} \\le \\frac{x^2}{2} \\le 2$<br/>Multiplying by $2$:<br/>$1 \\le x^2 \\le 4$.<br/>Taking the square root (considering both positive and negative solutions):<br/>$1 \\le |x| \\le 2$.<br/>This means $x \\in [-2, -1] \\cup [1, 2]$.</p>",
