    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: \\frac{-3\\pm\\sqrt{5}}{2}, \\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{5}}{2}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x)$ is an even function, $f(x) = f(-x)$.<br/>The equation is $f(x) = f\\left(\\frac{x+1}{x+2}\\right)$.<br/>Since $f$ is even, this implies either $x = \\frac{x+1}{x+2}$ or $x = -\\frac{x+1}{x+2}$.<br/>Case 1: $x = \\frac{x+1}{x+2} \\implies x^2 + 2x = x + 1 \\implies x^2 + x - 1 = 0$.<br/>The roots are $x = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4(-1)}}{2} = \\frac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}$.<br/>Case 2: $x = -\\frac{x+1}{x+2} \\implies x^2 + 2x = -x - 1 \\implies x^2 + 3x + 1 = 0$.<br/>The roots are $x = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{9 - 4(1)}}{2} = \\frac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}$.<br/>Since $\\sqrt{5} \\approx 2.236$, the roots are approximately $0.618, -1.618, -0.382, -2.618$.<br/>All of these lie in the interval $(-5, 5)$, which is the domain of $f$.<br/>Thus, the four real values are $\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{5}}{2}, \\frac{-1-\\sqrt{5}}{2}, \\frac{-3+\\sqrt{5}}{2}, \\frac{-3-\\sqrt{5}}{2}$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Hard"
