    "type": "subjective",
    "text": "Integrate $\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{4}}$ .",
    "images": [],
    "options": [],
    "correct": "\\frac{1}{2}\\tan^{-1} (x^2) + c",
    "chapter": "Indefinite Integrals",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: \\frac{1}{2}\\tan^{-1} (x^2) + c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $I = \\int \\frac{x}{1 + x^4} dx$.<br/>We can rewrite $x^4$ as $(x^2)^2$.<br/>Let $t = x^2$. Then $dt = 2x dx$, which means $x dx = \\frac{dt}{2}$.<br/>Substitute these into the integral:<br/>$I = \\int \\frac{1}{1 + t^2} \\frac{dt}{2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\int \\frac{1}{1 + t^2} dt$.<br/>The integral of $\\frac{1}{1 + t^2}$ is $\\tan^{-1}(t)$.<br/>So, $I = \\frac{1}{2} \\tan^{-1}(t) + C$.<br/>Substitute back $t = x^2$:<br/>$I = \\frac{1}{2} \\tan^{-1}(x^2) + C$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
