    "correct": "c",
    "chapter": "Inverse Trigonometric Functions",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are evaluating $\\sqrt{1+x^2} \\left( \\left[ x\\cos(\\cot^{-1}x) + \\sin(\\cot^{-1}x) \\right]^2 - 1 \\right)^{1/2}$ for $0 < x < 1$.<br/>Let $\\theta = \\cot^{-1}x$. Then $\\cot\\theta = x$.<br/>From a right triangle with adjacent $= x$ and opposite $= 1$, the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{x^2+1}$.<br/>Thus, $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}$ and $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}$.<br/>Substitute these into the inner expression:<br/>$x\\cos\\theta + \\sin\\theta = x\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}\\right) + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}} = \\frac{x^2+1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}} = \\sqrt{x^2+1}$.<br/>Square it and subtract 1:<br/>$(\\sqrt{x^2+1})^2 - 1 = x^2 + 1 - 1 = x^2$.<br/>Now take the power of $1/2$ (square root):<br/>$(x^2)^{1/2} = |x|$. Since $0 < x < 1$, $|x| = x$.<br/>Multiply by the leading term:<br/>$\\sqrt{1+x^2} \\cdot x = x\\sqrt{1+x^2}$.<br/>This corresponds to option c.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
