    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We want to evaluate $\\tan\\left(\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) - 2\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)\\right)$.<br/>Let $\\alpha = \\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right) \\implies \\sin\\alpha = \\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{3}{4}$.<br/>Let $\\beta = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right) \\implies \\cos\\beta = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$ and $\\tan\\beta = \\frac{1}{2}$.<br/>We need to find $\\tan(\\alpha - 2\\beta) = \\frac{\\tan\\alpha - \\tan 2\\beta}{1 + \\tan\\alpha \\tan 2\\beta}$.<br/>First, find $\\tan 2\\beta$:<br/>$\\tan 2\\beta = \\frac{2\\tan\\beta}{1 - \\tan^2\\beta} = \\frac{2(1/2)}{1 - (1/2)^2} = \\frac{1}{1 - 1/4} = \\frac{1}{3/4} = \\frac{4}{3}$.<br/>Now substitute into the tangent subtraction formula:<br/>$\\tan(\\alpha - 2\\beta) = \\frac{3/4 - 4/3}{1 + (3/4)(4/3)} = \\frac{(9 - 16)/12}{1 + 1} = \\frac{-7/12}{2} = -\\frac{7}{24}$.<br/>This corresponds to option b.</p>",
