    "type": "subjective",
    "text": "Prove that $cos \\left( {tan}^{-1} \\left( sin \\left( {cot}^{-1}x \\right) \\right) \\right) = \\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}+2}}$",
    "images": [],
    "options": [],
    "correct": "0",
    "chapter": "Inverse Trigonometric Functions",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 0</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are asked to analyze $\\cos(\\tan^{-1}(\\sin(\\cot^{-1} x)))$.<br/>Let $\\theta = \\cot^{-1} x$. Then $\\cot\\theta = x$.<br/>Using a right triangle with adjacent $= x$ and opposite $= 1$, the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{x^2+1}$.<br/>So, $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}$.<br/>Now we have $\\cos(\\tan^{-1}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}))$.<br/>Let $\\phi = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}\\right)$. Then $\\tan\\phi = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}$.<br/>Using a right triangle with opposite $= 1$ and adjacent $= \\sqrt{x^2+1}$, the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{1^2 + (\\sqrt{x^2+1})^2} = \\sqrt{x^2+2}$.<br/>Then $\\cos\\phi = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{x^2+1}}{\\sqrt{x^2+2}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2+2}}$.<br/>This proves that $\\cos(\\tan^{-1}(\\sin(\\cot^{-1} x))) = \\sqrt{\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2+2}}$.<br/>Note: If the given answer is 0, it may refer to the value of an expression derived from this identity being 0.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Hard"
