    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The given limit is $\\lim_{x\\rightarrow 1} \\left( \\frac{G(x)-G(1)}{x-1} \\right)$.<br/>By the definition of the derivative, this limit is exactly $G'(1)$.<br/>We have $G(x) = -\\sqrt{25 - x^2} = -(25 - x^2)^{1/2}$.<br/>Differentiate $G(x)$ with respect to $x$:<br/>$G'(x) = -\\frac{1}{2} (25 - x^2)^{-1/2} \\cdot (-2x) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{25 - x^2}}$.<br/>Substitute $x = 1$ into the derivative:<br/>$G'(1) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{25 - 1^2}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{24}}$.<br/>Thus, the value of the limit is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{24}}$. (Matches option a).</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
