    "correct": "b",
    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>For the function $f(x) = \\frac{\\ln(1+ax) - \\ln(1-bx)}{x}$ to be continuous at $x=0$, we must define $f(0)$ as the limit of $f(x)$ as $x \\to 0$.<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0} f(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\ln(1+ax) - \\ln(1-bx)}{x}$.<br/>We can split this into two standard limits by multiplying and dividing by the coefficients of $x$:<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left[ \\frac{\\ln(1+ax)}{ax} \\cdot a - \\frac{\\ln(1-bx)}{-bx} \\cdot (-b) \\right]$.<br/>Using the standard limit $\\lim_{u \\to 0} \\frac{\\ln(1+u)}{u} = 1$:<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0} f(x) = 1 \\cdot a - 1 \\cdot (-b) = a + b$.<br/>Therefore, the value that should be assigned to $f(0)$ is $a + b$. (Matches option b).</p>",
