    "correct": "c",
    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We need to evaluate the limit $L = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{g(x)f(a) - g(a)f(x)}{x - a}$.<br/>As $x \\to a$, the numerator approaches $g(a)f(a) - g(a)f(a) = 0$, and the denominator approaches $a - a = 0$.<br/>This is a $0/0$ indeterminate form. We can apply L'H&ocirc;pital's Rule by differentiating the numerator and the denominator with respect to $x$ (treating $a$ as a constant):<br/>$L = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{g'(x)f(a) - g(a)f'(x)}{1}$.<br/>Substitute $x = a$ into the expression:<br/>$L = g'(a)f(a) - g(a)f'(a)$.<br/>We are given: $f(a) = 2$, $f'(a) = 1$, $g(a) = -1$, and $g'(a) = 2$.<br/>Plug these values into the expression:<br/>$L = (2)(2) - (-1)(1) = 4 - (-1) = 4 + 1 = 5$.<br/>Thus, the value of the limit is $5$. (Matches option c).</p>",
