    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We want to evaluate $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left( \\frac{1}{1-n^2} + \\frac{2}{1-n^2} + \\dots + \\frac{n}{1-n^2} \\right)$.<br/>Since all terms have the same denominator, we can combine the numerators:<br/>$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1 + 2 + \\dots + n}{1 - n^2}$.<br/>The sum in the numerator is the sum of the first $n$ natural numbers, which is $\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$.<br/>So the expression becomes:<br/>$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n(n+1)}{2(1 - n^2)} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{n^2 + n}{2 - 2n^2}$.<br/>Divide the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $n$, which is $n^2$:<br/>$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{1 + \\frac{1}{n}}{\\frac{2}{n^2} - 2}$.<br/>As $n \\to \\infty$, $\\frac{1}{n} \\to 0$ and $\\frac{2}{n^2} \\to 0$.<br/>Thus, the limit is $\\frac{1 + 0}{0 - 2} = -\\frac{1}{2}$. (Matches option b).</p>",
