    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are asked to find $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\cos 2x)}}{x}$.<br/>Using the half-angle identity, we know that $1 - \\cos 2x = 2\\sin^2 x$.<br/>Substitute this into the expression:<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}(2\\sin^2 x)}}{x} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sqrt{\\sin^2 x}}{x}$.<br/>Since $\\sqrt{A^2} = |A|$, the numerator simplifies to $|\\sin x|$.<br/>The limit becomes $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{|\\sin x|}{x}$.<br/>Evaluate the Right-Hand Limit (RHL) as $x \\to 0^+$:<br/>$\\sin x > 0$, so $|\\sin x| = \\sin x$.<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0^+} \\frac{\\sin x}{x} = 1$.<br/>Evaluate the Left-Hand Limit (LHL) as $x \\to 0^-$:<br/>$\\sin x < 0$, so $|\\sin x| = -\\sin x$.<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0^-} \\frac{-\\sin x}{x} = -1$.<br/>Since LHL $\\neq$ RHL ($-1 \\neq 1$), the limit does not exist.<br/>This corresponds to \"none of these\". (Matches option d).</p>",
