    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = [x]^2 - [x^2]$.<br/>We test its continuity at integers. Since it involves $[x^2]$, we also need to be careful around integers.<br/>Consider $x = 0$:<br/>$f(0) = [0]^2 - [0^2] = 0$.<br/>RHL at 0: For $0 < x < 1$, $[x]=0$ and $[x^2]=0$, so $f(x) = 0 - 0 = 0$.<br/>LHL at 0: For $-1 < x < 0$, $[x]=-1$ and $[x^2]=0$, so $f(x) = (-1)^2 - 0 = 1$.<br/>LHL $\\neq$ RHL, so it is discontinuous at $x = 0$.<br/><br/>Consider $x = 1$:<br/>$f(1) = 1^2 - 1 = 0$.<br/>RHL at 1: For $1 < x < \\sqrt{2}$, $[x]=1$ and $[x^2]=1$, so $f(x) = 1^2 - 1 = 0$.<br/>LHL at 1: For $0 < x < 1$, $[x]=0$ and $[x^2]=0$, so $f(x) = 0 - 0 = 0$.<br/>Here LHL = RHL = $f(1) = 0$, so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 1$.<br/><br/>Consider $x = 2$:<br/>$f(2) = 2^2 - 4 = 0$.<br/>RHL at 2: For $2 < x < \\sqrt{5}$, $[x]=2$, $[x^2]=4 \\implies f(x) = 4 - 4 = 0$.<br/>LHL at 2: For $\\sqrt{3} < x < 2$, $[x]=1$, $[x^2]=3 \\implies f(x) = 1^2 - 3 = -2$.<br/>Discontinuous at $x = 2$.<br/>Similarly, for any integer $n \\neq 1$, it is discontinuous. Thus, it is discontinuous at all integers except 1.<br/>Matches option d.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Easy"
