    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{((a-n)nx - \\tan x)\\sin nx}{x^2} = 0$, where $n \\neq 0$.<br/>Rewrite the expression as:<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left( \\frac{(a-n)nx - \\tan x}{x} \\right) \\left( \\frac{\\sin nx}{x} \\right)$.<br/>Using the standard limit $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\sin nx}{x} = n$, we can extract it out since it's a finite non-zero value:<br/>$n \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{(a-n)nx - \\tan x}{x} = 0$.<br/>Since $n \\neq 0$, we must have:<br/>$\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\left( (a-n)n - \\frac{\\tan x}{x} \\right) = 0$.<br/>We know $\\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{\\tan x}{x} = 1$.<br/>So the limit becomes $(a-n)n - 1 = 0$.<br/>$(a-n)n = 1 \\implies a - n = \\frac{1}{n} \\implies a = n + \\frac{1}{n}$.<br/>Matches option d.</p>",
