    "correct": "d",
    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We evaluate $L = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{f(2h+2+h^2) - f(2)}{f(h-h^2+1) - f(1)}$.<br/>As $h \\to 0$, the numerator approaches $f(2) - f(2) = 0$ and the denominator approaches $f(1) - f(1) = 0$.<br/>This is a $0/0$ form, so we apply L'H&ocirc;pital's Rule by differentiating the numerator and the denominator with respect to $h$:<br/>Numerator derivative: $\\frac{d}{dh} [ f(2h+2+h^2) - f(2) ] = f'(2h+2+h^2) \\cdot (2 + 2h)$.<br/>Denominator derivative: $\\frac{d}{dh} [ f(h-h^2+1) - f(1) ] = f'(h-h^2+1) \\cdot (1 - 2h)$.<br/>Now take the limit as $h \\to 0$:<br/>Numerator becomes $f'(2) \\cdot (2 + 0) = 2f'(2)$.<br/>Denominator becomes $f'(1) \\cdot (1 - 0) = f'(1)$.<br/>Given $f'(2) = 6$ and $f'(1) = 4$, we get:<br/>$L = \\frac{2(6)}{4} = \\frac{12}{4} = 3$.<br/>Matches option d (which corresponds to 3).</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
