    "correct": "d",
    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The function is $f(x) = ||x| - 1|$.<br/>Non-differentiability typically occurs where the expressions inside absolute values change sign (i.e., are equal to 0).<br/>First, consider the inner absolute value, $|x|$. It is 0 when $x = 0$. Since the outer function does not smooth this out, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x = 0$.<br/>Second, consider the outer absolute value, which is 0 when $|x| - 1 = 0 \\implies |x| = 1 \\implies x = 1$ or $x = -1$.<br/>At $x = 1$ and $x = -1$, the function has sharp \"V\" shaped corners on the graph, causing the left and right derivatives to be different.<br/>Thus, the function is not differentiable at $x = -1, 0, 1$.<br/>The set of non-differentiable points is $\\{-1, 0, 1\\}$.<br/>Matches option d.</p>",
