    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let the limit be $L = \\lim_{x \\to 0^+} \\left( (\\sin x)^{1/x} + \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{\\sin x} \\right)$.<br/>Let's evaluate the two terms separately.<br/>First term: $L_1 = \\lim_{x \\to 0^+} (\\sin x)^{1/x}$.<br/>Since $\\sin x \\to 0^+$ and $1/x \\to \\infty$, this is of the form $0^\\infty$, which approaches 0. So $L_1 = 0$.<br/>Second term: $L_2 = \\lim_{x \\to 0^+} \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)^{\\sin x} = \\lim_{x \\to 0^+} \\exp\\left(\\sin x \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\right)$.<br/>$\\sin x \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\sin x \\ln x = -\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{x}\\right) (x \\ln x)$.<br/>We know that $\\lim_{x \\to 0^+} \\frac{\\sin x}{x} = 1$ and $\\lim_{x \\to 0^+} x \\ln x = 0$.<br/>Thus, the exponent approaches $-1 \\times 0 = 0$.<br/>Therefore, $L_2 = e^0 = 1$.<br/>The overall limit is $L = L_1 + L_2 = 0 + 1 = 1$.<br/>Matches option c.</p>",
