    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Evaluate the limit on the left-hand side:<br/>$L = \\lim_{x \\to 0} (1 + x \\ln(1+b^2))^{1/x}$.<br/>This is of the form $1^\\infty$. We use the property $\\lim_{x \\to 0} (1+kx)^{1/x} = e^k$.<br/>Here, $k = \\ln(1+b^2)$, so the limit is $e^{\\ln(1+b^2)} = 1+b^2$.<br/>We are given that $L = 2b \\sin^2\\theta$, so:<br/>$1+b^2 = 2b \\sin^2\\theta \\implies \\sin^2\\theta = \\frac{1+b^2}{2b}$.<br/>Since $b > 0$, by the AM-GM inequality, $1+b^2 \\ge 2b$, meaning $\\frac{1+b^2}{2b} \\ge 1$.<br/>However, we also know that $\\sin^2\\theta \\le 1$ for any real $\\theta$.<br/>The only way both inequalities can hold simultaneously is if they are both equal to 1.<br/>Therefore, $\\frac{1+b^2}{2b} = 1 \\implies 1+b^2 = 2b \\implies (b-1)^2 = 0 \\implies b = 1$.<br/>And $\\sin^2\\theta = 1 \\implies \\sin\\theta = \\pm 1$.<br/>Given $\\theta \\in (-\\pi, \\pi]$, the possible values for $\\theta$ are $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.<br/>Matches option d (which likely contains $\\pm \\pi/2$).</p>",
