    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We have the equation $(\\sqrt[3]{1+a}-1)x^2 + (\\sqrt{1+a}-1)x + (\\sqrt[6]{1+a}-1) = 0$.<br/>Let $1+a = t^6$. As $a \\to 0^+$, $t \\to 1^+$.<br/>Substitute $1+a = t^6$ into the equation:<br/>$((t^6)^{1/3} - 1)x^2 + ((t^6)^{1/2} - 1)x + ((t^6)^{1/6} - 1) = 0$<br/>$(t^2 - 1)x^2 + (t^3 - 1)x + (t - 1) = 0$.<br/>Since $t \\to 1^+$, $t \\neq 1$, so we can divide the entire equation by $(t-1)$:<br/>$(t+1)x^2 + (t^2+t+1)x + 1 = 0$.<br/>Now take the limit as $t \\to 1^+$:<br/>$(1+1)x^2 + (1^2+1+1)x + 1 = 0$<br/>$2x^2 + 3x + 1 = 0$.<br/>This is a quadratic equation with roots corresponding to the limits of $\\alpha(a)$ and $\\beta(a)$.<br/>Factor the quadratic:<br/>$2x^2 + 2x + x + 1 = 0 \\implies 2x(x+1) + 1(x+1) = 0 \\implies (2x+1)(x+1) = 0$.<br/>The roots are $x = -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x = -1$.<br/>Thus, $\\lim_{a \\to 0^+} \\alpha(a)$ and $\\lim_{a \\to 0^+} \\beta(a)$ are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-1$.<br/>Matches option b.</p>",
