    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b, d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The function is $f(x) = 1 + |\\sin x|$.<br/>Since $\\sin x$ is a continuous function for all $x$, and the absolute value function $|x|$ is also continuous, their composition $|\\sin x|$ is continuous everywhere. Adding a constant 1 does not affect continuity, so $f(x)$ is continuous everywhere.<br/>For differentiability, the function $|\\sin x|$ has sharp corners (non-differentiable points) wherever the expression inside the absolute value changes sign, i.e., where $\\sin x = 0$.<br/>$\\sin x = 0$ at $x = n\\pi$, where $n$ is any integer.<br/>At these points, the left-hand and right-hand derivatives are not equal (they are $\\pm 1$).<br/>Thus, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x = 0$ (since $0 \\cdot \\pi = 0$) and, in fact, not differentiable at an infinite number of points ($n\\pi$).<br/>This matches the descriptions in options b and d.</p>",
