    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a, b, c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = |x-3|$ for $x \\ge 1$, and $f(x) = \\frac{x^2}{4} - \\frac{3x}{2} + \\frac{13}{4}$ for $x < 1$.<br/>Check continuity at $x=1$:<br/>$f(1) = |1-3| = 2$.<br/>Left Hand Limit (LHL) at $x=1$ is $\\lim_{x \\to 1^-} \\left( \\frac{x^2}{4} - \\frac{3x}{2} + \\frac{13}{4} \\right) = \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{6}{4} + \\frac{13}{4} = \\frac{8}{4} = 2$.<br/>Since LHL $= f(1)$, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$.<br/><br/>Check differentiability at $x=1$:<br/>For $1 \\le x < 3$, $f(x) = -(x-3) = 3-x$. So the Right Hand Derivative (RHD) at $x=1$ is $-1$.<br/>For $x < 1$, $f'(x) = \\frac{2x}{4} - \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{3}{2}$. The Left Hand Derivative (LHD) at $x=1$ is $\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{3}{2} = -1$.<br/>Since LHD $= \\text{RHD} = -1$, $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=1$.<br/><br/>Check continuity and differentiability at $x=3$:<br/>For $x \\ge 1$, $f(x) = |x-3|$. This is clearly continuous at $x=3$.<br/>However, $|x-3|$ has a sharp corner at $x=3$, so the LHD is $-1$ and RHD is $1$. Thus, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=3$.<br/>This matches options that state $f$ is continuous at 1 and 3, and differentiable at 1 (options a, b, c).</p>",
