    "topic": "(With One Or More Than One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b, c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The area of an equilateral triangle with side length $s$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2$.<br/>We are given the area as $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}$. Thus:<br/>$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} s^2 = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\implies s^2 = 1$.<br/>The side length $s$ is the distance between the two vertices $(0,0)$ and $(x, g(x))$.<br/>$s^2 = (x - 0)^2 + (g(x) - 0)^2 = x^2 + (g(x))^2$.<br/>Since $s^2 = 1$, we have $x^2 + (g(x))^2 = 1$.<br/>Solving for $g(x)$, we get $(g(x))^2 = 1 - x^2$, which gives:<br/>$g(x) = \\sqrt{1 - x^2}$ or $g(x) = -\\sqrt{1 - x^2}$.<br/>Since both functions are defined on $(-1, 1)$ and satisfy the condition, both are valid choices for $g(x)$.<br/>Matches options b and c.</p>",
