    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a, c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $g(x) = 0$ for $x < a$; $g(x) = \\int_a^x f(t) dt$ for $a \\le x \\le b$; and $g(x) = \\int_a^b f(t) dt$ for $x > b$.<br/>Since $f(t) \\ge 1$, $g(x)$ is continuous everywhere because it's composed of constants and an integral of a continuous function, and the values match at the boundaries $x=a$ and $x=b$.<br/>Let's check differentiability at the boundaries.<br/>At $x=a$: LHD is $0$. RHD is $f(a) \\ge 1$. Since LHD $\\neq$ RHD, $g$ is not differentiable at $x=a$.<br/>At $x=b$: LHD is $f(b) \\ge 1$. RHD is $0$ (since the integral is a constant for $x > b$). Since LHD $\\neq$ RHD, $g$ is not differentiable at $x=b$.<br/>So $g(x)$ is continuous everywhere but not differentiable at $a$ and $b$.<br/>Matches options a, c.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
