    "correct": "0",
    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 0</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Evaluate $L = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\sqrt{\\frac{x-\\sin x}{x+\\cos^2 x}}$.<br/>Since the square root is a continuous function, we can evaluate the limit inside the square root first.<br/>Let $M = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{x-\\sin x}{x+\\cos^2 x}$.<br/>As $x \\to 0$, the numerator $x - \\sin x \\to 0 - 0 = 0$.<br/>The denominator $x + \\cos^2 x \\to 0 + \\cos^2(0) = 1$.<br/>Thus, $M = \\frac{0}{1} = 0$.<br/>Therefore, $L = \\sqrt{M} = \\sqrt{0} = 0$.</p>",
