    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: y = 2</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $|f(x_1) - f(x_2)| \\le (x_1 - x_2)^2$.<br/>Divide by $|x_1 - x_2|$ and take the limit as $x_2 \\to x_1$:<br/>$\\lim_{x_2 \\to x_1} \\left| \\frac{f(x_1) - f(x_2)}{x_1 - x_2} \\right| \\le \\lim_{x_2 \\to x_1} |x_1 - x_2|$.<br/>This implies $|f'(x_1)| \\le 0$.<br/>Since absolute value cannot be negative, $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x$.<br/>This means $f(x)$ is a constant function, let's say $y = C$.<br/>The tangent to a constant function at any point is the function itself, so the tangent line is $y = C$.<br/>We are given that the tangent passes through $(1, 2)$.<br/>Substituting $(1, 2)$ into $y = C$ gives $2 = C$.<br/>So the equation of the tangent is $y = 2$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
