    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: F</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The statement is: \"If $\\lim_{x \\to a} [f(x)g(x)]$ exists then both $\\lim_{x \\to a} f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x \\to a} g(x)$ exist.\"<br/>We can easily find a counterexample.<br/>Let $a = 0$.<br/>Let $f(x) = x$ and $g(x) = \\frac{1}{x}$.<br/>Then $f(x)g(x) = x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} = 1$ (for $x \\neq 0$).<br/>So $\\lim_{x \\to 0} [f(x)g(x)] = \\lim_{x \\to 0} 1 = 1$. The limit exists.<br/>However, $\\lim_{x \\to 0} g(x) = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{1}{x}$ does not exist.<br/>Thus, the given statement is False.</p>",
