    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 7</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>For $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we must have $k = \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{x^3 + x^2 - 16x + 20}{(x-2)^2}$.<br/>Substituting $x=2$ in the numerator gives $8 + 4 - 32 + 20 = 0$, so it is a $0/0$ form.<br/>We can use L'H&ocirc;pital's rule:<br/>$k = \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{3x^2 + 2x - 16}{2(x-2)}$.<br/>Substituting $x=2$ in the new numerator gives $12 + 4 - 16 = 0$. Still a $0/0$ form.<br/>Use L'H&ocirc;pital's rule again:<br/>$k = \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{6x + 2}{2}$.<br/>Now substituting $x=2$:<br/>$k = \\frac{6(2) + 2}{2} = \\frac{14}{2} = 7$.<br/>Thus, $k = 7$.</p>",
