    "id": "jee_adv_math_limits_continuity_and_differentiability_110",
    "number": "110",
    "type": "fill_in_the_blanks",
    "text": "Let $f(x)= \\left( \\begin{bmatrix} (x-1{)}^{2}sin \\frac{1}{x-1} -|x|, & if x\\neq 1 \\\\ -1 & , if x=1 \\end{bmatrix} \\right)$ be a real valued function. Then the set of points where $f(x)$ is not differentiable is ___",
    "images": [],
    "options": [],
    "correct": "\\{0\\}",
    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: \\{0\\}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $f(x) = (x-1)^2 \\sin\\frac{1}{x-1} - |x|$ for $x \\neq 1$, and $-1$ for $x = 1$.<br/>Let $g(x) = (x-1)^2 \\sin\\frac{1}{x-1}$ for $x \\neq 1$, and $0$ for $x = 1$.<br/>Then $f(x) = g(x) - |x|$.<br/>First, check the differentiability of $g(x)$ at $x=1$.<br/>$g'(1) = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{g(1+h) - g(1)}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{h^2 \\sin(1/h) - 0}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0} h \\sin(1/h) = 0$.<br/>Since the limit exists, $g(x)$ is differentiable at $x=1$, and by extension, differentiable everywhere else as it is a product of differentiable functions.<br/>The term $-|x|$ is continuous everywhere but not differentiable at $x = 0$.<br/>Since $g(x)$ is differentiable everywhere, the differentiability of $f(x)$ depends entirely on $-|x|$.<br/>Thus, $f(x)$ will fail to be differentiable exactly where $|x|$ fails to be differentiable, which is at $x=0$.<br/>The set of points where $f(x)$ is not differentiable is $\\{0\\}$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Hard"
