    "correct": "\\begin{cases} \\sqrt{4-x^2}, & -2 \\le x \\le 0 \\\\\\\\ -\\sqrt{4-x^2}, & 0 < x \\le 2 \\end{cases}",
    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: \\begin{cases} \\sqrt{4-x^2}, & -2 \\le x \\le 0 \\\\\\\\ -\\sqrt{4-x^2}, & 0 < x \\le 2 \\end{cases}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The equation $x^2 + y^2 = 4$ describes a circle of radius 2 centered at the origin.<br/>Solving for $y$, we get $y = \\pm \\sqrt{4 - x^2}$.<br/>To create a function $y = f(x)$ that is discontinuous, we can define it piecewise using the positive root for some interval and the negative root for another interval within the domain $[-2, 2]$.<br/>For example, we can switch from the upper semicircle to the lower semicircle at $x=0$:<br/>$f(x) = \\begin{cases} \\sqrt{4-x^2}, & -2 \\le x \\le 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{4-x^2}, & 0 < x \\le 2 \\end{cases}$.<br/>At $x=0$, the left hand limit is $\\sqrt{4} = 2$ and the right hand limit is $-\\sqrt{4} = -2$.<br/>Since the limits are not equal, the function is discontinuous at $x=0$.<br/>This perfectly satisfies the condition of being a discontinuous function derived from $x^2 + y^2 = 4$.</p>",
