    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 0, 1</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We are given $f(x) = \\frac{x}{1+e^{1/x}}$ for $x \\neq 0$, and $f(0) = 0$.<br/>The right hand derivative is:<br/>$f'(0^+) = \\lim_{h \\to 0^+} \\frac{f(h) - f(0)}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0^+} \\frac{\\frac{h}{1+e^{1/h}} - 0}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0^+} \\frac{1}{1+e^{1/h}}$.<br/>As $h \\to 0^+$, $1/h \\to \\infty$, so $e^{1/h} \\to \\infty$.<br/>Therefore, $f'(0^+) = \\frac{1}{\\infty} = 0$.<br/>The left hand derivative is:<br/>$f'(0^-) = \\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{f(h) - f(0)}{h} = \\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{1}{1+e^{1/h}}$.<br/>As $h \\to 0^-$, $1/h \\to -\\infty$, so $e^{1/h} \\to 0$.<br/>Therefore, $f'(0^-) = \\frac{1}{1+0} = 1$.<br/>*(Note: The provided answer key in the original question may be misaligned with another question. The derivatives are 0 and 1).*</p>",
