    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: -1</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We want to evaluate $\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{\\ln(1+2h) - 2\\ln(1+h)}{h^2}$.<br/>Using the Maclaurin series expansion for $\\ln(1+x)$ around $x=0$:<br/>$\\ln(1+x) = x - \\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^3}{3} - \\dots$<br/>Applying this to our terms:<br/>$\\ln(1+2h) = (2h) - \\frac{(2h)^2}{2} + O(h^3) = 2h - 2h^2 + O(h^3)$.<br/>$2\\ln(1+h) = 2\\left(h - \\frac{h^2}{2} + O(h^3)\\right) = 2h - h^2 + O(h^3)$.<br/>Substitute these into the numerator:<br/>$\\ln(1+2h) - 2\\ln(1+h) = (2h - 2h^2) - (2h - h^2) = -h^2$.<br/>Now, substituting this back into the limit:<br/>$\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{-h^2 + O(h^3)}{h^2} = -1$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
