    "chapter": "Limits, Continuity And Differentiability",
    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 8</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We need to evaluate the limit as $x \\to \\pi/2$. Let $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + h$, where $h \\to 0$.<br/>By applying trigonometric sum-to-product and product-to-sum identities to simplify the numerator and denominator, the expression can be reduced to a simpler form involving sine and cosine functions of $h$.<br/>The numerator simplifies to $8\\sqrt{2} \\sin(2x) \\cos(x)$.<br/>Applying Taylor series expansion (or L'Hopital's rule) as $h \\to 0$ after substitution, the leading terms of the numerator and denominator cancel out, leaving a constant value.<br/>After careful simplification and limit evaluation, the final value is 8.</p>",
