    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $\\Delta = \\begin{vmatrix} xp+y & x & y \\\\ yp+z & y & z \\\\ 0 & xp+y & yp+z \\end{vmatrix} = 0$.<br/>Apply the column operation $C_1 \\to C_1 - pC_2 - C_3$.<br/>For the first row, $C_1$ becomes: $(xp+y) - px - y = 0$.<br/>For the second row, $C_1$ becomes: $(yp+z) - py - z = 0$.<br/>For the third row, $C_1$ becomes: $0 - p(xp+y) - (yp+z) = -(px^2 + 2pxy + y^2) = -(px+y)p - (yp+z) = - (px^2 + 2pxy + py^2 + yz) $ (wait, evaluating this is easier expanding along $C_1$).<br/>The third element is $-(px^2 + 2pxy + z)$ No, it's $-(px^2+pxy+ypy+yz) = -(px^2+2py^2)$? Actually it is $-(x p^2 + 2 y p + z)$.<br/>Let's expand along the new first column:<br/>$\\Delta = -(x p^2 + 2 y p + z) \\begin{vmatrix} x & y \\\\ y & z \\end{vmatrix} = -(x p^2 + 2 y p + z) (xz - y^2) = 0$.<br/>Since this must hold for the condition in the options independent of $p$, we must have $xz - y^2 = 0$.<br/>$y^2 = xz \\implies x, y, z$ are in G.P.</p>",
