    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>For a homogeneous system of linear equations to have a non-zero (non-trivial) solution, the determinant of the coefficient matrix must be zero.<br/>The coefficient matrix is:<br/>$\\Delta = \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & -k & -1 \\\\ k & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\end{vmatrix} = 0$.<br/>Expanding the determinant along the first row:<br/>$1( (-1)(-1) - (-1)(1) ) - (-k)( k(-1) - (-1)(1) ) - 1( k(1) - (-1)(1) ) = 0$<br/>$1( 1 + 1 ) + k( -k + 1 ) - 1( k + 1 ) = 0$<br/>$2 - k^2 + k - k - 1 = 0$<br/>$1 - k^2 = 0 \\implies k^2 = 1$<br/>So, $k = \\pm 1$.<br/>The possible values of $k$ are $-1, 1$.</p>",
