    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The determinant equation is $\\begin{vmatrix} \\sin x & \\cos x & \\cos x \\\\ \\cos x & \\sin x & \\cos x \\\\ \\cos x & \\cos x & \\sin x \\end{vmatrix} = 0$.<br/>Apply the column operation $C_1 \\to C_1 + C_2 + C_3$. All elements in the first column become $\\sin x + 2\\cos x$.<br/>$\\Delta = (\\sin x + 2\\cos x) \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & \\cos x & \\cos x \\\\ 1 & \\sin x & \\cos x \\\\ 1 & \\cos x & \\sin x \\end{vmatrix} = 0$.<br/>Apply $R_2 \\to R_2 - R_1$ and $R_3 \\to R_3 - R_1$:<br/>$\\Delta = (\\sin x + 2\\cos x) \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & \\cos x & \\cos x \\\\ 0 & \\sin x - \\cos x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sin x - \\cos x \\end{vmatrix} = 0$.<br/>Expanding along the first column, we get:<br/>$(\\sin x + 2\\cos x)(\\sin x - \\cos x)^2 = 0$.<br/>So either $\\sin x - \\cos x = 0 \\implies \\tan x = 1$.<br/>Or $\\sin x + 2\\cos x = 0 \\implies \\tan x = -2$.<br/>We are given the interval $-\\pi/4 \\le x \\le \\pi/4$.<br/>In this interval, $\\tan x$ is strictly increasing and its range is $[-1, 1]$.<br/>Therefore, $\\tan x = -2$ has no solutions in this interval.<br/>The equation $\\tan x = 1$ has exactly one solution in this interval, which is $x = \\pi/4$.<br/>Thus, there is exactly 1 distinct real root.</p>",
