    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $A = \\begin{bmatrix} \\alpha & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.<br/>Calculate $A^2$:<br/>$A^2 = \\begin{bmatrix} \\alpha & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} \\alpha & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\alpha^2 + 0 & 0 + 0 \\\\ \\alpha + 1 & 0 + 1 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} \\alpha^2 & 0 \\\\ \\alpha + 1 & 1 \\end{bmatrix}$.<br/>Since $A^2 = B$, we equate corresponding elements:<br/>$\\alpha^2 = 1 \\implies \\alpha = \\pm 1$.<br/>$\\alpha + 1 = 5 \\implies \\alpha = 4$.<br/>Since there is no common value of $\\alpha$ that satisfies both conditions simultaneously, there is no real value of $\\alpha$ for which $A^2 = B$.</p>",
