    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let's find the characteristic equation of $A$, which is given by $|A - \\lambda I| = 0$.<br/>$|A - \\lambda I| = \\begin{vmatrix} 1-\\lambda & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} = (1-\\lambda) [ (1-\\lambda)(4-\\lambda) - (-2)(1) ] = 0$<br/>$(1-\\lambda) ( \\lambda^2 - 5\\lambda + 4 + 2 ) = (1-\\lambda) (\\lambda^2 - 5\\lambda + 6) = 0$<br/>$-\\lambda^3 + 6\\lambda^2 - 11\\lambda + 6 = 0 \\implies \\lambda^3 - 6\\lambda^2 + 11\\lambda - 6 = 0$.<br/>By the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation. Thus:<br/>$A^3 - 6A^2 + 11A - 6I = 0$.<br/>Multiply the equation by $A^{-1}$:<br/>$A^2 - 6A + 11I - 6A^{-1} = 0 \\implies 6A^{-1} = A^2 - 6A + 11I$.<br/>Comparing this with $6A^{-1} = A^2 + cA + dI$, we get $c = -6$ and $d = 11$.<br/>Thus, $(c,d) = (-6, 11)$.</p>",
