    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $b_{ij} = 2^{i+j} a_{ij}$.<br/>The matrix $Q$ can be written by multiplying the rows and columns of $P$ by powers of 2.<br/>Let $D = \\text{diag}(2^1, 2^2, 2^3)$.<br/>Then $Q = D P D$.<br/>Taking the determinant on both sides:<br/>$|Q| = |D P D| = |D| \\cdot |P| \\cdot |D| = |D|^2 |P|$.<br/>Since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, its determinant is the product of its diagonal elements:<br/>$|D| = 2^1 \\times 2^2 \\times 2^3 = 2^{1+2+3} = 2^6$.<br/>We are given $|P| = 2$.<br/>Therefore, $|Q| = (2^6)^2 \\times 2 = 2^{12} \\times 2 = 2^{13}$.</p>",
