    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $P = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 1 \\end{bmatrix} = I + N$, where $N = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.<br/>Calculate $N^2$:<br/>$N^2 = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.<br/>Calculate $N^3$:<br/>$N^3 = N^2 \\cdot N = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.<br/>Since $N^3 = 0$, $N$ is a nilpotent matrix.<br/>We can expand $P^{50}$ using the binomial theorem since $I$ and $N$ commute:<br/>$P^{50} = (I + N)^{50} = I + 50N + \\frac{50 \\times 49}{2} N^2 = I + 50N + 1225N^2$.<br/>We are given $P^{50} - Q = I \\implies Q = P^{50} - I = 50N + 1225N^2$.<br/>$Q = 50 \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} + 1225 \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 200 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 800 & 200 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 19600 & 0 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.<br/>$Q = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 200 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 20400 & 200 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$.<br/>From this, we identify $q_{31} = 20400$, $q_{32} = 200$, and $q_{21} = 200$.<br/>The required value is $\\frac{q_{31} + q_{32}}{q_{21}} = \\frac{20400 + 200}{200} = \\frac{20600}{200} = 103$.</p>",
