    "correct": "d",
    "chapter": "Permutations And Combinations",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The set $S = \\{1, 2, \\dots, 9\\}$ contains 5 odd numbers (1, 3, 5, 7, 9) and 4 even numbers (2, 4, 6, 8).<br/>We want to form subsets of 5 elements having exactly $k$ odd numbers.<br/>The number of such subsets is choosing $k$ odd numbers from 5, and the remaining $(5-k)$ even numbers from 4.<br/>Thus, $N_k = \\binom{5}{k} \\binom{4}{5-k}$.<br/>$N_1 = \\binom{5}{1}\\binom{4}{4} = 5 \\times 1 = 5$<br/>$N_2 = \\binom{5}{2}\\binom{4}{3} = 10 \\times 4 = 40$<br/>$N_3 = \\binom{5}{3}\\binom{4}{2} = 10 \\times 6 = 60$<br/>$N_4 = \\binom{5}{4}\\binom{4}{1} = 5 \\times 4 = 20$<br/>$N_5 = \\binom{5}{5}\\binom{4}{0} = 1 \\times 1 = 1$<br/>The sum $N_1 + N_2 + N_3 + N_4 + N_5 = 5 + 40 + 60 + 20 + 1 = 126$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
