    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a, b, d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$n_1$ is the number of triplets $(i,j,k)$ where elements are in $\\{1,\\dots,10\\}$. Since repetition is allowed, $n_1 = 10 \\times 10 \\times 10 = 1000$. (Option a is True).<br/>$n_2$ is the number of pairs $(i,j)$ such that $1 \\le i \\le j-1$ and $j \\le 8$.<br/>For a given $j$, $i$ can take $j-1$ values. Summing over $j=2$ to 8:<br/>$1 + 2 + 3 + \\dots + 7 = \\frac{7 \\times 8}{2} = 28$.<br/>Wait, the condition is $j+2 \\le 10 \\implies j \\le 8$. And $1 \\le i < j+2 \\implies i \\le j+1$.<br/>Number of values for $i$ for a given $j$ is $j+1$.<br/>Sum over $j=1$ to $8$: $\\sum_{j=1}^8 (j+1) = 2 + 3 + \\dots + 9 = \\frac{9 \\times 10}{2} - 1 = 44$. (Option b is True).<br/>$n_3$ is the number of 4-tuples with $1 \\le i < j < k < l \\le 10$, which is choosing 4 elements out of 10 without replacement and order doesn't matter: $\\binom{10}{4} = 210$. (Option c is False, states 220).<br/>$n_4$ is the number of 4-tuples with distinct elements, which is ${}^{10}P_4 = 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7 = 5040$.<br/>$n_4 / 12 = 5040 / 12 = 420$. (Option d is True).<br/>Correct options are a, b, d.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Hard"
