    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The three given lines are:<br/>1) $2x + y - 3 = 0$<br/>2) $5x + ky - 3 = 0$<br/>3) $3x - y - 2 = 0$<br/>If they are concurrent, they must meet at a single point. Let's find the intersection of line 1 and line 3.<br/>Adding 1) and 3):<br/>$(2x + y - 3) + (3x - y - 2) = 0 \\implies 5x - 5 = 0 \\implies x = 1$.<br/>Substitute $x = 1$ in 1):<br/>$2(1) + y - 3 = 0 \\implies y = 1$.<br/>So the point of intersection is $(1, 1)$.<br/>For the lines to be concurrent, $(1, 1)$ must lie on the second line:<br/>$5(1) + k(1) - 3 = 0$<br/>$k + 2 = 0 \\implies k = -2$.</p>",
