    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The original line is $3x - 4y + 7 = 0$, which has slope $m_1 = \\frac{3}{4}$.<br/>It is rotated clockwise by $\\pi/4$ about the point $(1, 5/2)$. Note that this point lies on the line since $3(1) - 4(5/2) + 7 = 0$.<br/>Let the slope of the new line be $m_2$.<br/>Since it's rotated clockwise, $m_2 = \\tan(\\theta - \\pi/4)$, where $\\tan\\theta = 3/4$.<br/>$m_2 = \\frac{\\tan\\theta - \\tan(\\pi/4)}{1 + \\tan\\theta \\tan(\\pi/4)} = \\frac{3/4 - 1}{1 + (3/4)(1)} = \\frac{-1/4}{7/4} = -\\frac{1}{7}$.<br/>The new line passes through $(1, 5/2)$ with slope $-1/7$.<br/>$y - \\frac{5}{2} = -\\frac{1}{7}(x - 1)$<br/>$7y - \\frac{35}{2} = -x + 1$<br/>Multiply by 2:<br/>$14y - 35 = -2x + 2 \\implies 2x + 14y - 37 = 0$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Easy"
