    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The equations of the diagonals of the parallelogram $PQRS$ are $x + 3y = 4$ and $6x - 2y = 7$.<br/>The slope of the first diagonal is $m_1 = -\\frac{1}{3}$.<br/>The slope of the second diagonal is $m_2 = -\\frac{6}{-2} = 3$.<br/>Let's find the product of their slopes:<br/>$m_1 m_2 = \\left( -\\frac{1}{3} \\right)(3) = -1$.<br/>Since the product of their slopes is -1, the diagonals intersect at right angles (they are perpendicular).<br/>A parallelogram whose diagonals are perpendicular to each other is a rhombus.<br/>*(It could also be a square if the diagonals are equal in length, but we cannot deduce that here, so it \"must\" be a rhombus).*</p>",
