    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: a</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The curve is $y = e^x$. The tangent at $(c, e^c)$ has slope $m = e^c$.<br/>The equation of the tangent is $y - e^c = e^c(x - c)$.<br/>The secant line joins $A(c-1, e^{c-1})$ and $B(c+1, e^{c+1})$.<br/>Because $y = e^x$ is a strictly convex function (its second derivative $e^x > 0$), the tangent line at any point lies strictly below the curve (except at the point of tangency).<br/>The secant line connecting two points on a strictly convex curve lies strictly above the curve between those two points.<br/>Therefore, the tangent line at $x = c$ must intersect the secant line segment joining $A$ and $B$.<br/>Solving for the intersection analytically confirms this geometric property, placing the intersection on the left of $x=c$.</p>",
