    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The three lines forming the triangle are:<br/>$L_1: (1+p)x - py + p(1+p) = 0 \\implies$ x-intercept is $-p$<br/>$L_2: (1+q)x - qy + q(1+q) = 0 \\implies$ x-intercept is $-q$<br/>$L_3: y = 0$ (the x-axis).<br/>Two vertices are $A(-p, 0)$ and $B(-q, 0)$.<br/>The third vertex $C$ is the intersection of $L_1$ and $L_2$.<br/>Solving $L_1$ and $L_2$, we get $x = pq$ and $y = (1+p)(1+q)$. So $C$ is $(pq, (1+p)(1+q))$.<br/>The altitude from $C$ to $AB$ (which is on $y=0$) is the vertical line $x = pq$.<br/>The slope of $L_2$ (which is $AC$) is $\\frac{1+q}{q}$.<br/>The altitude from $A(-p, 0)$ to $AC$ has slope $-\\frac{q}{1+q}$.<br/>Equation of this altitude: $y - 0 = -\\frac{q}{1+q}(x + p)$.<br/>Substitute $x = pq$ to find the orthocentre:<br/>$y = -\\frac{q}{1+q}(pq + p) = -\\frac{pq(1+q)}{1+q} = -pq$.<br/>The orthocentre is $(x, y) = (pq, -pq)$.<br/>Therefore, $y = -x$, or $x + y = 0$. This is a straight line.</p>",
