    "chapter": "Points, Straight Lines And Family Of Straight Lines",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: x + 5y = \\pm 5\\sqrt{2}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The given line is $5x - y = 1$, which has a slope of $5$.<br/>The line $L$ is perpendicular to it, so its slope is $-1/5$.<br/>Let the equation of line $L$ be $x + 5y = c$.<br/>The x-intercept is $c$ (when $y=0$) and the y-intercept is $c/5$ (when $x=0$).<br/>The line forms a right-angled triangle with the coordinate axes, with base $|c|$ and height $|c/5|$.<br/>The area of this triangle is $5$ sq. units.<br/>Area $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} |c| \\left|\\frac{c}{5}\\right| = \\frac{c^2}{10}$.<br/>So, $\\frac{c^2}{10} = 5 \\implies c^2 = 50 \\implies c = \\pm \\sqrt{50} = \\pm 5\\sqrt{2}$.<br/>Thus, the equation of the line $L$ is $x + 5y = \\pm 5\\sqrt{2}$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Hard"
