    "chapter": "Points, Straight Lines And Family Of Straight Lines",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: T</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The vertices of the triangle are $A(6, 3), B(-3, 5)$ and $C(4, -2)$.<br/>Let's find the area of $\\Delta ABC$ using the determinant formula:<br/>Area $= \\frac{1}{2} |6(5 - (-2)) + (-3)(-2 - 3) + 4(3 - 5)|$<br/>$= \\frac{1}{2} |6(7) - 3(-5) + 4(-2)| = \\frac{1}{2} |42 + 15 - 8| = \\frac{49}{2}$.<br/>Now let's find the area of $\\Delta PBC$ for any point $P(x, y)$:<br/>Area $= \\frac{1}{2} |x(5 - (-2)) + (-3)(-2 - y) + 4(y - 5)|$<br/>$= \\frac{1}{2} |7x + 6 + 3y + 4y - 20| = \\frac{1}{2} |7x + 7y - 14| = \\frac{7}{2} |x + y - 2|$.<br/>The ratio of the area of $\\Delta PBC$ to $\\Delta ABC$ is:<br/>$\\frac{\\frac{7}{2} |x + y - 2|}{\\frac{49}{2}} = \\frac{7 |x + y - 2|}{49} = \\frac{|x + y - 2|}{7}$.<br/>Thus, ignoring the absolute value depending on the position of P relative to BC, the expression is $\\frac{x + y - 2}{7}$. This proves the statement.</p>",
