    "chapter": "Points, Straight Lines And Family Of Straight Lines",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: (0, 5/2) \\text{ or } (0,0)</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The sides of the rhombus have slopes $m_1 = 1$ and $m_2 = 7$.<br/>The diagonals of a rhombus bisect the interior angles between its sides.<br/>The angle bisectors for lines with slopes 1 and 7 passing through the origin (for simplicity to find slopes) are $y-x=0$ and $7x-y=0$.<br/>$\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\pm \\frac{7x-y}{\\sqrt{50}} \\implies 5(y-x) = \\pm (7x-y)$.<br/>Taking $+$: $5y - 5x = 7x - y \\implies 6y = 12x \\implies y = 2x$ (slope 2).<br/>Taking $-$: $5y - 5x = -7x + y \\implies 4y = -2x \\implies y = -x/2$ (slope -1/2).<br/>The diagonals of the actual rhombus have these slopes and pass through the intersection point $(1, 2)$.<br/>Equation of $D_1$: $y - 2 = 2(x - 1) \\implies y = 2x$.<br/>Equation of $D_2$: $y - 2 = -1/2(x - 1) \\implies 2y - 4 = -x + 1 \\implies x + 2y = 5$.<br/>Vertex $A$ lies on the y-axis (so $x = 0$) and on one of the diagonals.<br/>If $A$ is on $D_1$: $y = 2(0) = 0$. So $A$ is $(0, 0)$.<br/>If $A$ is on $D_2$: $0 + 2y = 5 \\implies y = 5/2$. So $A$ is $(0, 5/2)$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Easy"
