    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: Check Answer Key</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>This is a classic vector geometry proof.<br/>Let the position vectors of the vertices of $\\Delta ABC$ and $\\Delta PQR$ be $\\vec{a}, \\vec{b}, \\vec{c}$ and $\\vec{p}, \\vec{q}, \\vec{r}$.<br/>The condition that perpendiculars from $A, B, C$ to $QR, RP, PQ$ are concurrent translates to an algebraic identity involving dot products.<br/>By algebraic manipulation (using the property $\\vec{x} \\cdot (\\vec{y} - \\vec{z})$ etc.), this identity is completely symmetric with respect to both triangles.<br/>Therefore, the symmetric relation implies that the perpendiculars from $P, Q, R$ to $BC, CA, AB$ must also be concurrent.</p>",
