    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: T</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>We find the point of intersection of the lines $x + 2y - 10 = 0$ and $2x + y + 5 = 0$.<br/>Multiply the first equation by 2: $2x + 4y - 20 = 0$.<br/>Subtract the second equation: $(2x + 4y - 20) - (2x + y + 5) = 0 \\implies 3y - 25 = 0 \\implies y = 25/3$.<br/>Substitute $y$: $x + 2(25/3) - 10 = 0 \\implies x + 50/3 - 30/3 = 0 \\implies x = -20/3$.<br/>The point of intersection is $(-20/3, 25/3)$.<br/>Now check if this point lies on the line $5x + 4y = 0$:<br/>$5(-20/3) + 4(25/3) = -100/3 + 100/3 = 0$.<br/>Since it satisfies the equation, the statement is True.</p>",
