    "chapter": "Points, Straight Lines And Family Of Straight Lines",
    "topic": "True/False",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: T</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let the lines be $L_1: 2x + 3y + 19 = 0$ and $L_2: 9x + 6y - 17 = 0$.<br/>$L_1$ cuts the axes at $A(-19/2, 0)$ and $B(0, -19/3)$.<br/>$L_2$ cuts the axes at $C(17/9, 0)$ and $D(0, 17/6)$.<br/>Four points on the axes are concyclic if the product of their x-intercepts equals the product of their y-intercepts (by intersecting chords theorem).<br/>Product of x-intercepts: $OA \\times OC = \\left(-\\frac{19}{2}\\right) \\times \\frac{17}{9} = -\\frac{323}{18}$.<br/>Product of y-intercepts: $OB \\times OD = \\left(-\\frac{19}{3}\\right) \\times \\frac{17}{6} = -\\frac{323}{18}$.<br/>Since $OA \\times OC = OB \\times OD$, the points $A, B, C, D$ are concyclic.<br/>The statement is True.</p>",
