    "correct": "(i) \\frac{1}{132}, (ii) \\frac{1}{462}, (iii) \\frac{1}{1260}",
    "chapter": "Probability",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: (i) \\frac{1}{132}, (ii) \\frac{1}{462}, (iii) \\frac{1}{1260}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Total ways to arrange 6 boys and 6 girls is $12!$.<br/>(i) 6 girls sit together: Treat them as 1 block. 6 boys + 1 block = 7 items. Arranged in $7!$ ways. Girls arranged internally in $6!$ ways. Prob = $\\frac{7! 6!}{12!} = \\frac{1}{132}$.<br/>(ii) Boys and girls alternate: Two patterns (BGBG... or GBGB...). Each pattern has $6! \\times 6!$ ways. Total = $2 \\times 6! \\times 6!$. Prob = $\\frac{2 \\times 6! \\times 6!}{12!} = \\frac{2 \\times 720}{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7} = \\frac{1}{462}$.<br/>(iii) The third part was not in the text, but the provided answer is $1/1260$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
