    "correct": "infinite",
    "chapter": "Sequences And Series",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: infinite</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let the terms be $a r^p = 27$, $a r^q = 8$, $a r^s = 12$.<br/>Then $r^{p-q} = 27/8 = (3/2)^3$.<br/>$r^{s-q} = 12/8 = 3/2$.<br/>So $r^{p-q} = (r^{s-q})^3 = r^{3(s-q)}$.<br/>This implies $p - q = 3(s - q) \\implies p - 3s + 2q = 0$.<br/>We can find infinitely many integer triplets $(p, q, s)$ that satisfy this equation (for instance, let $q=0, s=1, p=3$).<br/>For each such triplet, we can choose $r$ suitably (e.g. $r = (3/2)^{1/(s-q)}$).<br/>Thus, there exist infinitely many such geometric progressions.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
