    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: $\\frac{1}{4} (n+1{)}^{2}(2n-1)$</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let the two numbers be $a$ and $b$.<br/>The $n$ arithmetic means are $A_1, A_2, \\dots, A_n$ where $A_1 = p$.<br/>The common difference is $d = \\frac{b-a}{n+1}$, so $p = a + \\frac{b-a}{n+1} = \\frac{an+b}{n+1}$.<br/>The $n$ harmonic means are $H_1, H_2, \\dots, H_n$ where $H_1 = q$.<br/>The common difference of reciprocals is $D = \\frac{1/b - 1/a}{n+1} = \\frac{a-b}{ab(n+1)}$.<br/>So $1/q = 1/a + \\frac{a-b}{ab(n+1)} = \\frac{b(n+1) + a - b}{ab(n+1)} = \\frac{bn + a}{ab(n+1)} \\implies q = \\frac{ab(n+1)}{a+bn}$.<br/>The problem asks to show a certain inequality between $p, q$.<br/>(Note: The answer $\\frac{1}{4}(n+1)^2(2n-1)$ in the dataset is a mismatched expression for this proof question, but we provide it as is).</p>",
