    "correct": "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}",
    "chapter": "Sequences And Series",
    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The angles subtended by the sides at the center are $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$. Their sum is $360^\\circ$ or $2\\pi$.<br/>We want the minimum of A.M. of $\\cos(\\alpha + \\pi/2), \\cos(\\beta + \\pi/2), \\cos(\\gamma + \\pi/2)$, which are $-\\sin\\alpha, -\\sin\\beta, -\\sin\\gamma$.<br/>So we want the minimum of $\\frac{-(\\sin\\alpha + \\sin\\beta + \\sin\\gamma)}{3}$.<br/>We want the maximum of $\\sin\\alpha + \\sin\\beta + \\sin\\gamma$ subject to $\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = 2\\pi$.<br/>Let the angles of the triangle be $A, B, C$. The central angles are $2A, 2B, 2C$. So $\\alpha = 2A, \\beta = 2B, \\gamma = 2C$.<br/>$\\sin 2A + \\sin 2B + \\sin 2C = 4 \\sin A \\sin B \\sin C$.<br/>For a triangle, the maximum of $\\sin A \\sin B \\sin C$ is attained when $A = B = C = 60^\\circ$.<br/>In this case, the maximum is $4 \\times (\\sqrt{3}/2)^3 = 4 \\times \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<br/>Thus, $\\sin\\alpha + \\sin\\beta + \\sin\\gamma \\le \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.<br/>The minimum A.M. is $\\frac{-1}{3} (\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.</p>",
