    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$2\\sin^2\\theta - 3\\sin\\theta - 2 = 0$.<br/>Let $y = \\sin\\theta$. $2y^2 - 3y - 2 = 0 \\implies 2y^2 - 4y + y - 2 = 0 \\implies 2y(y - 2) + 1(y - 2) = 0$.<br/>$(2y + 1)(y - 2) = 0$.<br/>$y = -1/2$ or $y = 2$.<br/>Since $\\sin\\theta$ must be between -1 and 1, $y = 2$ is rejected.<br/>$\\sin\\theta = -1/2$.<br/>The general solution for $\\sin\\theta = \\sin\\alpha$ is $\\theta = n\\pi + (-1)^n \\alpha$.<br/>Here $\\alpha = -\\pi/6$.<br/>So $\\theta = n\\pi + (-1)^n (-\\pi/6) = n\\pi + (-1)^{n+1} \\pi/6$. Option d.</p>",
