    "chapter": "Trigonometric Equations",
    "topic": "Subjective Problems",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{3}</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$(1 - \\tan\\theta)(1 + \\tan\\theta) \\sec^2\\theta + 2^{\\tan^2\\theta} = 0$.<br/>$(1 - \\tan^2\\theta)(1 + \\tan^2\\theta) + 2^{\\tan^2\\theta} = 0$.<br/>$1 - \\tan^4\\theta + 2^{\\tan^2\\theta} = 0$.<br/>Let $u = \\tan^2\\theta \\ge 0$. Then $1 - u^2 + 2^u = 0 \\implies 2^u = u^2 - 1$.<br/>For $u=0$, $1 = -1$ (False).<br/>For $u=1$, $2 = 0$ (False).<br/>For $u=2$, $4 = 3$ (False).<br/>For $u=3$, $8 = 8$ (True).<br/>So $u = 3$ is a solution. $\\tan^2\\theta = 3 \\implies \\tan\\theta = \\pm \\sqrt{3}$.<br/>In $(-\\pi/2, \\pi/2)$, $\\theta = \\pm \\pi/3$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Easy"
