    "chapter": "Trigonometric Equations",
    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 2</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Let $E = \\sin^2\\theta + 3\\sin\\theta \\cos\\theta + 5\\cos^2\\theta$.<br/>$E = \\frac{1-\\cos 2\\theta}{2} + \\frac{3}{2} \\sin 2\\theta + 5 \\frac{1+\\cos 2\\theta}{2} = 3 + 2\\cos 2\\theta + \\frac{3}{2}\\sin 2\\theta$.<br/>The minimum value of $E$ is $3 - \\sqrt{2^2 + (3/2)^2} = 3 - \\sqrt{4 + 9/4} = 3 - \\sqrt{25/4} = 3 - 5/2 = 1/2$.<br/>The maximum value of the given expression $\\frac{1}{E}$ is $\\frac{1}{\\min(E)} = \\frac{1}{1/2} = 2$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
