    "correct": "7",
    "chapter": "Trigonometric Equations",
    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 7</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$\\frac{1}{\\sin(\\pi/n)} - \\frac{1}{\\sin(3\\pi/n)} = \\frac{1}{\\sin(2\\pi/n)}$.<br/>$\\frac{\\sin(3\\pi/n) - \\sin(\\pi/n)}{\\sin(\\pi/n)\\sin(3\\pi/n)} = \\frac{1}{\\sin(2\\pi/n)}$.<br/>$2\\sin(\\pi/n)\\cos(2\\pi/n) \\sin(2\\pi/n) = \\sin(\\pi/n)\\sin(3\\pi/n)$.<br/>$2\\cos(2\\pi/n)\\sin(2\\pi/n) = \\sin(3\\pi/n) \\implies \\sin(4\\pi/n) = \\sin(3\\pi/n)$.<br/>Since $n > 3$, $4\\pi/n$ and $3\\pi/n$ cannot be equal.<br/>Thus, they must be supplementary: $4\\pi/n + 3\\pi/n = \\pi \\implies 7\\pi/n = \\pi \\implies n = 7$.</p>",
