    "chapter": "Trigonometric Equations",
    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 8</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Equation: $\\frac{5}{4} \\cos^2 2x + (\\cos^4 x + \\sin^4 x) + (\\cos^6 x + \\sin^6 x) = 2$.<br/>We know $\\cos^4 x + \\sin^4 x = 1 - 2\\sin^2 x \\cos^2 x = 1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin^2 2x$.<br/>$\\cos^6 x + \\sin^6 x = 1 - 3\\sin^2 x \\cos^2 x = 1 - \\frac{3}{4}\\sin^2 2x$.<br/>Substitute these in:<br/>$\\frac{5}{4} \\cos^2 2x + 1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin^2 2x + 1 - \\frac{3}{4}\\sin^2 2x = 2$.<br/>$\\frac{5}{4} \\cos^2 2x - \\frac{5}{4}\\sin^2 2x = 0$.<br/>$\\cos^2 2x = \\sin^2 2x \\implies \\tan^2 2x = 1 \\implies \\tan 2x = \\pm 1$.<br/>$2x = n\\pi \\pm \\pi/4 = (4n \\pm 1)\\pi/4$.<br/>$x = (4n \\pm 1)\\pi/8$.<br/>In $[0, 2\\pi]$, $x = \\pi/8, 3\\pi/8, 5\\pi/8, 7\\pi/8, 9\\pi/8, 11\\pi/8, 13\\pi/8, 15\\pi/8$.<br/>Total 8 solutions.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
