    "type": "numerical",
    "text": "Let $a,b,c$ be three non-zero real numbers such that the equation $\\sqrt{3} acosx+2bsinx=c,x\\in \\left( - \\frac{\\pi }{2} , \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\right)$ , has two distinct real roots $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ with $\\alpha +\\beta = \\frac{\\pi }{3}$ . Then, the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$ is ___ .",
    "images": [],
    "options": [],
    "correct": "0.5",
    "chapter": "Trigonometric Equations",
    "topic": "Numerical/Integer Value Type Questions",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: 0.5</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$\\sqrt{3}a\\cos x + 2b\\sin x = c$.<br/>Roots are $\\alpha, \\beta$. $\\sqrt{3}a\\cos\\alpha + 2b\\sin\\alpha = c$ and $\\sqrt{3}a\\cos\\beta + 2b\\sin\\beta = c$.<br/>Subtracting them: $\\sqrt{3}a(\\cos\\alpha - \\cos\\beta) + 2b(\\sin\\alpha - \\sin\\beta) = 0$.<br/>$\\sqrt{3}a (-2\\sin\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2}\\sin\\frac{\\alpha-\\beta}{2}) + 2b (2\\cos\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2}\\sin\\frac{\\alpha-\\beta}{2}) = 0$.<br/>Since $\\alpha \\neq \\beta$, $\\sin\\frac{\\alpha-\\beta}{2} \\neq 0$.<br/>$-\\sqrt{3}a \\sin\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2} + 2b \\cos\\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2} = 0$.<br/>Given $\\alpha+\\beta = \\pi/3 \\implies \\frac{\\alpha+\\beta}{2} = \\pi/6$.<br/>$-\\sqrt{3}a \\sin(\\pi/6) + 2b \\cos(\\pi/6) = 0$.<br/>$-\\sqrt{3}a (1/2) + 2b (\\sqrt{3}/2) = 0$.<br/>$-\\sqrt{3}a/2 + \\sqrt{3}b = 0 \\implies a/2 = b \\implies b/a = 1/2 = 0.5$.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
    "difficulty": "Medium"
