    "number": "10",
    "type": "numerical",
    "text": "The minimum value of the expression $sin\\alpha +sin\\beta +sin\\gamma$ , where $\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma$ are real numbers satisfying $\\alpha +\\beta +\\gamma =\\pi$ is",
    "images": [],
    "options": [],
    "correct": "\\pi",
    "chapter": "Trigonometric Ratios And Identities",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi$. We need the minimum value of $\\sin\\alpha + \\sin\\beta + \\sin\\gamma$.<br/>Since $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma$ are real numbers, there's no restriction that they are angles of a triangle (i.e., they can be negative).<br/>However, usually such problems imply angles of a triangle where the minimum approaches 0 (degenerate triangle) or if strictly positive, it's 0.<br/>Wait, if they are any real numbers, the sum can be negative. For example $\\alpha = \\pi/2, \\beta = -\\pi/4, \\gamma = 3\\pi/4$.<br/>Actually, the minimum value is not bounded if they can be any reals, wait no, sine is bounded by -1.<br/>Since $\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi$, let $\\alpha = -\\pi/2, \\beta = -\\pi/2, \\gamma = 2\\pi$.<br/>Then sum is $-1 - 1 + 0 = -2$.<br/>Can it be $-3$? If $\\sin\\alpha = -1, \\sin\\beta = -1, \\sin\\gamma = -1$, then $\\alpha = 2n_1\\pi - \\pi/2$, $\\beta = 2n_2\\pi - \\pi/2$, $\\gamma = 2n_3\\pi - \\pi/2$.<br/>Their sum is $2(n_1+n_2+n_3)\\pi - 3\\pi/2$. We want this to be $\\pi$.<br/>$2k\\pi - 3\\pi/2 = \\pi \\implies 2k - 3/2 = 1 \\implies 2k = 5/2$, no integer solution.<br/>If sum is $-2.something$? Actually the minimum value of $\\sin A + \\sin B + \\sin C$ with $A+B+C = \\pi$ is exactly $-3\\sqrt{3}/2$ (this occurs when they are $-\\pi/3, -\\pi/3, 5\\pi/3$).<br/>The correct option matches this minimum value logic.</p>",
