    "chapter": "Trigonometric Ratios And Identities",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>Given $\\sin\\theta = 1/2 \\implies \\theta = \\pi/6$ (since $\\theta$ is acute).<br/>$\\cos\\phi = 1/3$.<br/>We know $\\cos(\\pi/3) = 1/2$ and $\\cos(\\pi/2) = 0$.<br/>Since $0 < 1/3 < 1/2$, the angle $\\phi$ is between $\\pi/3$ and $\\pi/2$.<br/>$\\theta + \\phi = \\pi/6 + \\phi$.<br/>$\\pi/6 + \\pi/3 < \\theta + \\phi < \\pi/6 + \\pi/2$.<br/>$\\pi/2 < \\theta + \\phi < 2\\pi/3$.<br/>So $\\theta + \\phi \\in (\\pi/2, 2\\pi/3)$, which corresponds to option b.</p>",
    "needsManualReview": false,
