    "chapter": "Trigonometric Ratios And Identities",
    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: d</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>For set P: $\\sin\\theta - \\cos\\theta = \\sqrt{2}\\cos\\theta \\implies \\sin\\theta = (\\sqrt{2} + 1)\\cos\\theta \\implies \\tan\\theta = \\sqrt{2} + 1$.<br/>For set Q: $\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta = \\sqrt{2}\\sin\\theta \\implies \\cos\\theta = (\\sqrt{2} - 1)\\sin\\theta \\implies \\tan\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2} - 1} = \\sqrt{2} + 1$.<br/>Both sets yield the same condition $\\tan\\theta = \\sqrt{2} + 1$.<br/>Therefore, the sets $P$ and $Q$ are equal, $P = Q$. Option d.</p>",
