    "topic": "Fill In The Blanks",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: \\left[0, \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\right] \\cup \\left\\{\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right\\} \\cup \\left[\\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\pi\\right]</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$2\\sin^2 x - 3\\sin x + 1 \\ge 0 \\implies (2\\sin x - 1)(\\sin x - 1) \\ge 0$.<br/>Since $\\sin x \\le 1$ always, $\\sin x - 1 \\le 0$.<br/>For the product to be $\\ge 0$, we must have $2\\sin x - 1 \\le 0$ OR $\\sin x - 1 = 0$.<br/>Case 1: $2\\sin x - 1 \\le 0 \\implies \\sin x \\le 1/2$.<br/>In $[0, \\pi]$, $\\sin x \\le 1/2 \\implies x \\in [0, \\pi/6] \\cup [5\\pi/6, \\pi]$.<br/>Case 2: $\\sin x = 1 \\implies x = \\pi/2$.<br/>Combining these, $x \\in [0, \\pi/6] \\cup \\{\\pi/2\\} \\cup [5\\pi/6, \\pi]$.</p>",
