    "topic": "Multiple Choice Questions (With One Correct Answer)",
    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: b</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>$\\vec{p}, \\vec{q}, \\vec{r}$ are mutually perpendicular and have same magnitude, say $k$.<br/>$\\vec{p} \\times ((\\vec{x} - \\vec{q}) \\times \\vec{p}) = (\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{p})(\\vec{x} - \\vec{q}) - (\\vec{p} \\cdot (\\vec{x} - \\vec{q}))\\vec{p}$.<br/>Since $\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{q} = 0$, this is $k^2 (\\vec{x} - \\vec{q}) - (\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{x})\\vec{p}$.<br/>Similarly for the other terms.<br/>Summing them up:<br/>$k^2 [(\\vec{x} - \\vec{q}) + (\\vec{x} - \\vec{r}) + (\\vec{x} - \\vec{p})] - (\\vec{p}\\cdot\\vec{x})\\vec{p} - (\\vec{q}\\cdot\\vec{x})\\vec{q} - (\\vec{r}\\cdot\\vec{x})\\vec{r} = 0$.<br/>Since $\\vec{p}/k, \\vec{q}/k, \\vec{r}/k$ form an orthonormal basis, $\\vec{x} = (\\vec{x} \\cdot \\frac{\\vec{p}}{k})\\frac{\\vec{p}}{k} + \\dots$<br/>So $(\\vec{p}\\cdot\\vec{x})\\vec{p} + (\\vec{q}\\cdot\\vec{x})\\vec{q} + (\\vec{r}\\cdot\\vec{x})\\vec{r} = k^2 \\vec{x}$.<br/>Thus $k^2 [3\\vec{x} - (\\vec{p} + \\vec{q} + \\vec{r})] - k^2 \\vec{x} = 0$.<br/>$2\\vec{x} = \\vec{p} + \\vec{q} + \\vec{r} \\implies \\vec{x} = \\frac{1}{2}(\\vec{p} + \\vec{q} + \\vec{r})$. Option b.</p>",
