    "solution": "<p><strong>Ans: c</strong></p><p><strong>Step-by-step Solution:</strong><br/>The vectors are coplanar if their scalar triple product is zero.<br/>$\\begin{vmatrix} -\\lambda^2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda^2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -\\lambda^2 \\end{vmatrix} = 0$.<br/>Let $x = -\\lambda^2$. The determinant is $\\begin{vmatrix} x & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & x \\end{vmatrix} = (x+2)(x-1)^2 = 0$.<br/>So $x = 1$ or $x = -2$.<br/>$-\\lambda^2 = 1 \\implies \\lambda^2 = -1$ (no real solution for $\\lambda$).<br/>$-\\lambda^2 = -2 \\implies \\lambda^2 = 2 \\implies \\lambda = \\pm \\sqrt{2}$.<br/>There are two distinct real values of $\\lambda$. Option c gives 2.</p>",
